{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:29.932Z","ordinal":12105,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0eef853664611d0c016a927b1bdbf087d091f0ced6a414e2cb","status":"completed","call_id":"call_Id0CQqMmsdDwy696ABaFCywO","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd:\"set -e\\nwc -l /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\\nsed -n '241,520p' /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\\nnl -ba /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | sed -n '691,748p'\\nsed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md\",\n  workdir:\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\",\n  yield_time_ms:10000,\n  max_output_tokens:30000\n});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","create_time":1787984661.413196}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:30.045Z","ordinal":12106,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af","turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c7703b9e-d2ee-4ce5-9abb-46362b0ce82d","process_id":"32162","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","set -e\nwc -l /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\nsed -n '241,520p' /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\nnl -ba /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | sed -n '691,748p'\nsed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"set -e\nwc -l /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\nsed -n '241,520p' /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\nnl -ba /Users/serro/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md | sed -n '691,748p'\nsed -n '1,240p' /Users/serro/.codex/memories/rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"     500 /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\n  small multiples. Render every requested dimension simultaneously; never hide\n  one behind a toggle.\n\n### Maps\n\n- Let the map dominate the composition. Use at most one compact\n  selection/detail area and only requested controls.\n- Always project published GeoJSON/TopoJSON and sourced longitude/latitude with\n  `d3-geo`; never hard-code or hand-draw geographic outlines. Use schematic maps\n  only when asked.\n- For world countries, import\n  `https://esm.sh/@d3-maps/atlas@1.0.0/world/countries/countries-110m` and convert\n  it with `topojson-client@3.1.0` using\n  `feature(world, world.objects.features).features`. Join input ISO3 directly to\n  `feature.properties.id`, which is already ISO3; do not convert it to numbers.\n- For US states or counties, use\n  `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/us-atlas@3/counties-10m.json/+esm`. For ZIP/ZCTA\n  or city boundaries, download official Census or local open-data GeoJSON; do\n  not guess sibling atlas paths or import raw JSON as JavaScript.\n- Keep maps geographically legible: for local points, fetch published\n  neighborhood, street, or comparable geometry; a blank field or lone\n  administrative outline is not a basemap. Show the full city or region behind\n  points or partial choropleths, and frame the locations with modest padding.\n- Include the verified geometry in the final HTML. Open it before replying and\n  fix blank basemaps, failed imports, missing labels, or unprojected points.\n\n### Dense categorical grid\n\n- Use one compact horizontal selected-item summary, then a grid with exactly one\n  readable identifier per cell, then one small legend. Render only that\n  identifier as visible cell text; put all other metadata in an accessible label\n  or one summary line, not badges or fact grids. Allow only selection unless\n  asked.\n\n### Part-to-whole or time allocation\n\n- Use compact metrics and one stacked chart of category allocation per period.\n  Never substitute totals-only bars or duplicate it as a heatmap and totals\n  chart.\n\n## Layout and accessibility\n\n- Use semantic HTML, keyboard-accessible controls, and concise labels.\n- Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` for dynamic results, selections, and simulator\n  updates. Use `role=\"alert\"` for validation errors. Do not announce every\n  hover or animation frame.\n- Keep the top-level surface transparent and unframed, and fill the available\n  conversation width. Design for 736px, or 1,024px in wide mode, and support\n  widths down to 320px. Stack side-by-side content when it no longer fits.\n- At every supported width, text, controls, cards, toolbars, and dynamic content\n  must fit without overlap or clipping. Reflow by stacking or wrapping; use\n  `.table-responsive` only when table columns cannot fit. The host sizes the\n  frame to its content, so avoid fixed outer widths, other horizontal overflow,\n  internal scrolling, `position: fixed`, and viewport-height layouts.\n- Size every SVG from its actual container. At narrow widths, reduce ticks,\n  declutter annotations, and keep visible text at least 11 screen pixels;\n  never shrink a fixed-width `viewBox`.\n- Keep native tab order; never add `tabindex`.\n- Use native `button`, `input`, `select`, and `textarea` elements with matching\n  utilities; never recreate controls.\n- Keep browser or utility focus styles; never override them.\n- On coarse pointers, provide non-overlapping effective targets about 44px by\n  44px without breaking 320px layouts; visible icons and marks may stay small.\n  Keep fine-pointer controls compact, and let shared utilities own touch sizing\n  and at least 16px editable-field text.\n- Keep essential content and actions available without hover.\n\n## Typography\n\n- Scale type with `--font-size-base`. Use normal text by default and\n  `.text-small` only for secondary annotations (never below 11px).\n- `h1`, `h2`, and `h3` are available; use one concise visible heading for a\n  self-contained chart or graph, with short panel headings only when needed.\n  Do not restate the prompt or add a redundant title to other visualizations.\n- Use only weights `400` and `500`. Never set custom font sizes or line heights.\n- Use `.tabular-nums` on changing or aligned numbers. Avoid it for editorial or\n  decorative numerals.\n\n## Color\n\n- Make every fill, stroke, text, border, shadow, chart, and canvas color\n  theme-aware. Never hardcode light or dark palettes such as white panels,\n  off-white backgrounds, black text, slate strokes, or Tailwind color literals.\n- Keep text readable against its actual background. Muted or secondary colors\n  must retain clear contrast; never use `.text-muted` inside `.card` or another\n  filled container unless its background preserves that contrast.\n- Available theme variables include `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`,\n  `--card-foreground`, `--popover`, `--popover-foreground`, `--primary`,\n  `--primary-foreground`, `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--muted`,\n  `--muted-foreground`, `--accent`, `--accent-foreground`, `--destructive`,\n  `--border`, `--input`, `--ring`, `--blue`, `--orange`, `--green`, `--red`,\n  `--purple`, and `--yellow`. Use `currentColor` inside SVG.\n- Never add decorative borders, outlines, or strokes to progress tracks,\n  meters, bars, stacked segments, or other filled quantitative marks. Use a\n  subtle neutral or translucent track and distinguish marks with fill,\n  contrast, spacing, or opacity.\n- Use `--viz-series-1` for one measure or active state. Use `--viz-series-2`\n  through `--viz-series-6` only for important persistent category, series, or\n  status identity; never give every peer a different color by default.\n  - For categorical tiles or nodes, prefer a soft low-opacity series fill with a\n    neutral or transparent border; never color every outline.\n  - Keep mappings stable and pair color with labels, shapes, or line styles.\n  - Secondary series colors are theme-derived; never assume hues or use them\n    decoratively.\n- When color encodes a category or series, apply it consistently to the\n  corresponding visual marks—not just the legend—and keep large-area fills\n  subtle.\n- Use series colors only for chart lines, marks, and legend swatches. Keep\n  values, axis text, and direct labels in `--foreground` or\n  `--muted-foreground`.\n- Keep chart grids and inactive structure thin and neutral. Use 1-2px neutral\n  structural paths; never thicken, dash, or double-stroke the whole structure.\n- In each color pair, the base token is a surface and its\n  `-foreground` token is the content on that surface. Use `.btn-primary` for\n  high-emphasis actions; its neutral fill is supplied by the utility. Use\n  `--primary` and `--primary-foreground` for filled selected, active, or pressed\n  controls. Reserve `--accent` and `--accent-foreground` for subtle interactive\n  surfaces and soft highlights. Buttons with\n  `aria-pressed=\"true\"`, `aria-selected=\"true\"`, or `.is-selected` already use\n  the primary pairing.\n\n## Design system\n\n- Let utilities own geometry, appearance, and interaction. Use the matching\n  utility for every button and form control. Never restyle utilities,\n  descendants, or pseudo-elements: no custom sizes, spacing, borders, radii,\n  shadows, colors, or interaction states.\n\n### Surfaces and layout\n\n- `.card`: The only card-like HTML surface. Use its base class unchanged for a\n  necessary numeric summary, selected-item summary, or bounded interactive\n  field. Before adding a fill, border, radius, or shadow to any layout container,\n  either use `.card` or leave it transparent and unframed; never recreate card\n  chrome on rows, panels, tiles, sections, or wrappers. Keep charts, maps,\n  diagrams, tables, controls, and the whole visualization unframed. Never nest\n  cards; show 2-4 summaries near the top only when useful. Structural groupings\n  and repeated content are not bounded interactive fields. Organize them with\n  layout or visual marks, not container chrome.\n- `.viz-stat`: Use a summary `.card` with one muted label, one\n  `.viz-stat-value`, and at most one short context or delta line.\n- `.viz-grid`: Use for peer metrics or choices instead of a custom grid. It\n  creates as many equal-width columns as fit and stacks when narrow. Never use it\n  for the whole visual or a horizontally scrolling card row. Keep groups to 2-3\n  columns at 736px and controls in a separate row.\n- `.viz-row`: Use as a wrapping horizontal group with centered related values or\n  inline actions that may wrap when narrow.\n- `.progress` + `.progress-bar`:\n  `<div class=\"progress\" role=\"progressbar\" aria-label=\"Progress\" aria-valuenow=\"25\" aria-valuemin=\"0\" aria-valuemax=\"100\"><div class=\"progress-bar\" style=\"width:25%\"></div></div>`\n- `.viz-tile`: Add to a selectable dense-grid `.btn`; it stretches to fill its\n  grid cell, preserves category fill, and uses an accent ring instead of solid\n  selection. Never add another selected, pressed, border, outline, or shadow\n  rule.\n- `.viz-badge`: Use as a compact display-only accent pill for a short status,\n  category, or value; never as a button.\n- `.viz-controls`: Use as a wrapping row for controls affecting the same\n  visualization. Keep button groups compact. Put labeled fields directly inside\n  as `.form-label`; fields form at most two columns and stack when narrow.\n\n### Controls\n\n- `.btn`: Use for a content-sized secondary action. Add `.btn-primary` for one\n  main action per control group or `.btn-ghost` for low emphasis.\n- `.btn-block`: Add to a `.btn` only when the action should intentionally fill\n  the available inline space. Never use it for ordinary row actions.\n- `<a>`: Use for links. Add `.btn` to style a link as a button.\n- `[data-tooltip]`: Use for concise supplementary plain text on static or dynamic\n  triggers; the sandbox handles hover, focus, and touch and creates `.tooltip`\n  elements. Keep essential content visible and triggers labeled. Never use\n  `title`, custom markup, or initialization. Example:\n  `<button type=\"button\" data-tooltip=\"Reset view\">Reset</button>`.\n- `[data-tooltip-placement]`: Optionally prefer `top` (default), `right`,\n  `bottom`, or `left`; collision handling may flip it.\n- `.form-check`: Prefer a wrapping `<label class=\"form-check\">` around the\n  native `.form-check-input` and `.form-check-label` text so the whole row is\n  tappable. An explicit label with matching `for` and input `id` also works.\n- `.form-switch`: Add to `.form-check` around a native checkbox.\n- `.form-control`: Pair a native text, date, file, or color input—or a\n  textarea—with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-control-color`: Add to `.form-control` for a compact native color\n  input.\n- `.form-select`: Pair a native select with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-range`: Pair a native range with a visible label; put its current value\n  and units immediately before it.\n\n### Tables\n\n- `.table`: Use on a semantic table for a quiet, unframed data view. It provides\n  wrapping cells and subtle horizontal dividers without vertical gridlines. Use\n  sentence case for headers.\n- `.table-responsive`: Wrap a table when its columns cannot fit at narrow\n  widths. It contains horizontal overflow without clipping the visualization.\n- `.table-sm`: Add to `.table` when more rows need to fit; it reduces cell\n  padding without shrinking text.\n- `.text-end`, `.text-center`, and `.text-nowrap`: Use inside `.table` for\n  numeric/end alignment, centered values, or values that must stay on one line.\n  Numeric cells use tabular figures when end-aligned.\n\n### Text\n\n- `.text-small`: Use for the smallest host-scaled secondary chart labels and\n  annotations, never below 11px or for essential content.\n- `.text-muted`: Use for secondary units, captions, timestamps, and context,\n  never essential values or labels.\n- `.text-destructive`: Use only for error or validation text the user needs to\n  notice or act on.\n- `<code>`: Use for inline commands, file names, symbols, or short references;\n  put multiline code in `<pre><code>`.\n- `.sr-only`: Use for visually hidden accessible text.\n\n## Charts\n\n- Prefer inline SVG for simple charts and version-pinned approved-CDN\n  libraries when native interaction, scales, legends, or layout materially\n  improve the result.\n- Resolve theme colors before passing them to canvas or chart APIs that cannot\n  parse CSS variables or `light-dark(...)`; redraw when the theme changes.\n- Use a tooltip unless it would distract from a simple, directly labeled chart.\n  Keep chart-library tooltips and grouped legend interactions native; never\n  replace them with a custom one-point tooltip. For SVG, attach `data-tooltip`\n  directly to the real pointer-accessible mark and include its label, value,\n  and units; the sandbox handles themed positioning, keyboard focus, and touch.\n- Animate transitions between chart states so lines and marks move to their new\n  values, resampling paths when point counts differ. Do not animate initial\n  appearance or use fade-only effects; never loop motion, and honor\n  `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Scope SVG styles to the chart class. Never target every `svg` in a container\n  that also contains Lucide icons.\n- Include labeled axes, units, and directly labeled important values. Give every\n  chart, SVG, canvas, and widget a concise screen-reader summary using a role and\n  accessible name or description, SVG `<title>`/`<desc>`, fallback text, or an\n  `.sr-only` heading or description.\n- Reserve space for the longest formatted label at every supported width. Axis\n  ticks are secondary and may use `.text-small` when space is tight. Never\n  overlap or clip text against marks, axes, legends, labels, or edges; move or\n  reduce labels rather than squeeze them.\n- Add a legend only when multiple series cannot be labeled directly.\n- Pair color with shape or text so meaning never depends on color alone.\n\n## Icons and mockups\n\n- Use the sandbox-provided global `lucide`. Add an icon name with `data-lucide`:\n\n  ```html\n  <i data-lucide=\"search\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\n  ```\n\n- Lucide replaces the placeholder in place with an inline SVG. Icons are 16px\n  and inherit `currentColor`.\n- Mark decorative icons `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. Put action icons inside labeled\n  controls; use a visible label or `aria-label` for icon-only actions.\n- Let the sandbox initialize static icons after the fragment without blocking\n  first render. After adding icons dynamically, use\n  `lucide.createIcons({ attrs: { width: 16, height: 16 } })`.\n- Never load Lucide or another icon library from the network.\n- Use visibly labeled buttons and inputs for small interactions. Keep all\n  presentation-only interaction local to the fragment and make the first render\n  useful before input changes.\n- Use semantic controls, realistic spacing, and restrained chrome for mockups.\n  Never fake product screenshots when inspectable UI is needed.\n   691\tscope: Use for DATA-1/AUTH-1-style canonical-gateway issue delivery from clean `origin/dev` worktrees through bounded review, exact-head merge, and cleanup.\n   692\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=worktree, approval, and exact-head procedures are reusable, but code, PR, CI, and deployment evidence are snapshot-specific.\n   693\t\n   694\t## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees, success\n   695\t\n   696\t### rollout_summary_files\n   697\t\n   698\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, DATA-1 and AUTH-1 implemented, merged, and cleaned up)\n   699\t\n   700\t### keywords\n   701\t\n   702\t- DATA-1, AUTH-1, canonical-gateway, origin/dev, 72aabce, placement, CAS, HMAC assertions, replay protection, single-writer-strong, PR #121, PR #120, 1bf6f6bb\n   703\t\n   704\t## Task 2: Refetch and classify AUTH-1 PR review feedback once, success\n   705\t\n   706\t### rollout_summary_files\n   707\t\n   708\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, all 14 unresolved threads were stale or already fixed)\n   709\t\n   710\t### keywords\n   711\t\n   712\t- splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1, GH_CONFIG_DIR, reviewThreads, isOutdated, exact PR head, --match-head-commit, bf3a19d1, Node 22\n   713\t\n   714\t## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 then DATA-1 into dev, success\n   715\t\n   716\t### rollout_summary_files\n   717\t\n   718\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, exact-head merges completed and final origin/dev verified)\n   719\t\n   720\t### keywords\n   721\t\n   722\t- gh pr merge, --match-head-commit, origin/dev, git ort, 454c40f8, 3158fc83, e3b9834c, npm --workspace datafn/server test, git diff --check\n   723\t\n   724\t## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees, success\n   725\t\n   726\t### rollout_summary_files\n   727\t\n   728\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, dedicated worktrees removed without touching others)\n   729\t\n   730\t### keywords\n   731\t\n   732\t- git worktree remove, DATA-1, AUTH-1, process timeout, git worktree list, cleanup\n   733\t\n   734\t## User preferences\n   735\t\n   736\t- when the user requests new worktrees “off `origin/dev`” -> preserve the dirty checkout, create an isolated worktree from the authoritative exact ref, and keep requested scopes separate. [Task 1]\n   737\t- when the user requests end-to-end work in new worktrees -> preserve the dirty checkout, use freshly fetched `origin/dev` worktrees, and carry publication/verification through only when authorized. [Task 1]\n   738\t- when the user invokes `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> perform one bounded round: unresolved GraphQL threads first, then reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; validate against the exact worktree; push at most once; compare heads once; stop without post-push polling. [Task 2]\n   739\t\n   740\t## Reusable knowledge\n   741\t\n   742\t- DATA-1 PR #121 and AUTH-1 PR #120 were merged into `dev`; placement ownership remains separate from permission-grant projections/outboxes, while AUTH-1 keeps direct-mode compatibility alongside canonical gateway routing. [Task 1][Task 3]\n   743\t- DynamoDB placement uses `single-writer-strong` consistency and consistent reads; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic. Routing assertions bind identity plus relevant method/path/body data; stale routing may retry only once before execution. [Task 1]\n   744\t- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`. Review every unresolved thread against `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head; timestamp-only comment queries are insufficient. [Task 2]\n   745\t- Exact merge pattern: merge the first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch resulting `origin/dev`, merge it into the dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, reread PR head, merge with an exact-head guard, then verify ancestry in `origin/dev`. DATA-1 validation: 92 files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 3]\n   746\t\n   747\t## Failures and how to do differently\n   748\t\nthread_id: 01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af\nupdated_at: 2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00\nrollout_path: /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl\ncwd: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\ngit_branch: SFNS-2\n\n# DATA-1 and AUTH-1 were implemented, reviewed, merged, and their dedicated worktrees removed\n\nRollout context: The user requested end-to-end work on Linear issues DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in separate worktrees based on freshly fetched `origin/dev`, followed by PR review remediation, merging, and cleanup. The original dirty checkout `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev` was preserved. Hosted Skillplane workflow `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1` was retrieved and followed.\n\n## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user asked to “work DATA-1 and AUTH-1 issues in a new worktrees off of origin/dev end to end” -> preserve the existing checkout, use separate clean worktrees from the authoritative fetched ref, and keep issue scopes isolated.\n- The workflow required local implementation and verification before publication approval, then exact issue-ID branches for publication.\n\nKey steps:\n- Read both complete Linear issues, all comments, linked architecture/rationale documents, relations, and project metadata.\n- Fetched `origin/dev` at `72aabce915426325450663feffd7a53089ef4489` and created detached worktrees under `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1` and `AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 implemented tenant placement separate from permission indexing, atomic placement/CAS epoch moves, gateway dispatch, signed assertions, pre-execution fencing, bounded retry, sync/WebSocket behavior, migration fencing, observability, provider-neutral adapters, tests, and documentation.\n- AUTH-1 implemented canonical gateway routing with direct-mode compatibility, identity placement/CAS, signed HMAC assertions and replay protection, pre-side-effect routing, TypeScript/Python adapter support, OAuth/session semantics, deletion fencing, migration behavior, Swift/client updates, tests, and documentation.\n- DATA-1 was published as PR #121 and AUTH-1 as PR #120. DATA-1 later received one narrow remediation fixing placement validation order before custom-route middleware; commit `1bf6f6bb` was pushed and verified against the PR head.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- The original checkout was dirty with unrelated `.conduct` artifacts; do not clean or modify it during similar work.\n- AUTH-1’s initial remote CI had unrelated repository failures, including a FastAPI `StopIteration` issue in prior history; distinguish local feature validation from repository-wide CI and live multi-region proof.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Placement ownership must remain a separate source of truth from permission-grant projections/outboxes.\n- Routing assertions should bind request identity and relevant method/path/body data, and stale routing may retry only once before execution.\n- DynamoDB placement used `single-writer-strong` consistency; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic.\n\nReferences:\n- Hosted workflow: `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1`, digest prefix `716e55…abaf`.\n- Worktrees: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`, `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 PR #121: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/121`.\n- AUTH-1 PR #120: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/120`.\n\n## Task 2: Bounded AUTH-1 PR review remediation\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user invoked `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> use one bounded review round: fetch unresolved GraphQL threads plus reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; classify against the exact PR worktree; push at most once; verify heads once; do not refetch or poll afterward.\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 worktree was clean and local, remote, and PR heads matched `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- Frozen review snapshot contained 56 threads, 14 unresolved, 10 with current comments, 4 outdated-only threads, 49 reviews, 4 issue comments, 54 inline comments, and 28 checks.\n- Verified all unresolved findings against current code. Middleware execution, `/runtime` compatibility/global classification, cell-region registration precedence, deletion fencing, error propagation, and related issues were already fixed or stale. No edit, commit, or push was needed.\n- Recorded unrelated failed CI causes: DevFn required Node 22 but CI used Node 20; BillFn and SQLAlchemy had pre-existing typing issues; python-core had Ruff import formatting failures.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Do not treat every unresolved GitHub thread as actionable; inspect `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head.\n- Do not use timestamp-only comment queries; unresolved historical threads can remain actionable, while many current-looking threads are stale.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`.\n- Use exact-head guards such as `--match-head-commit`; after an authorized push, compare local, remote, and live PR SHAs once and stop.\n\nReferences:\n- Skill: `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md`.\n- AUTH-1 final reviewed head: `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- AUTH-1 PR review result: 0 fixes; all 14 unresolved threads stale/already fixed.\n\n## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 and DATA-1 into dev\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed both PRs were clean and mergeable; merge commits were enabled.\n- Merged AUTH-1 first with an exact-head guard. PR #120 merged at `REDACTED`.\n- Fetched the resulting `origin/dev` and merged it into DATA-1. Git’s `ort` merge completed without conflicts; no manual conflict resolution was required.\n- Ran the combined DATA-1 server suite: 92 test files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; DataFn typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Pushed DATA-1 merge commit `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`, verified local/remote/PR heads, then merged PR #121 at `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Verified both PR heads are ancestors of final `origin/dev` `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Refresh a dependent PR against the actual post-merge `origin/dev` before merging it. This avoided guessing about overlap and confirmed whether conflicts existed.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Exact merge pattern: inspect PR state/heads, merge first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch `origin/dev`, merge into dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, re-read PR head, merge with exact-head guard, and verify ancestry in `origin/dev`.\n\nReferences:\n- AUTH-1 merge commit: `REDACTED`.\n- DATA-1 branch merge commit before PR merge: `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`.\n- Final `origin/dev`: `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Validation: `npm --workspace datafn/server test`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run typecheck`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run build`, `git diff --check`.\n\n## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 and DATA-1 worktrees were clean and registered.\n- Ran Git worktree removal for both paths. The first command exceeded its execution window, but the process was checked and allowed to finish rather than racing a second cleanup.\n- Verified both directories were absent, Git no longer listed them, and local branches remained available: AUTH-1 at `bf3a19d1…`, DATA-1 at `3158fc83…`.\n- Other worktrees were untouched.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- `git worktree remove` may take longer than the command timeout on large repositories. Check for an active process before retrying.\n\nReferences:\n- Removed: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1` and `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`.\n- Remaining branches: `AUTH-1`, `DATA-1`.\n\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"     500 /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\n  small multiples. Render every requested dimension simultaneously; never hide\n  one behind a toggle.\n\n### Maps\n\n- Let the map dominate the composition. Use at most one compact\n  selection/detail area and only requested controls.\n- Always project published GeoJSON/TopoJSON and sourced longitude/latitude with\n  `d3-geo`; never hard-code or hand-draw geographic outlines. Use schematic maps\n  only when asked.\n- For world countries, import\n  `https://esm.sh/@d3-maps/atlas@1.0.0/world/countries/countries-110m` and convert\n  it with `topojson-client@3.1.0` using\n  `feature(world, world.objects.features).features`. Join input ISO3 directly to\n  `feature.properties.id`, which is already ISO3; do not convert it to numbers.\n- For US states or counties, use\n  `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/us-atlas@3/counties-10m.json/+esm`. For ZIP/ZCTA\n  or city boundaries, download official Census or local open-data GeoJSON; do\n  not guess sibling atlas paths or import raw JSON as JavaScript.\n- Keep maps geographically legible: for local points, fetch published\n  neighborhood, street, or comparable geometry; a blank field or lone\n  administrative outline is not a basemap. Show the full city or region behind\n  points or partial choropleths, and frame the locations with modest padding.\n- Include the verified geometry in the final HTML. Open it before replying and\n  fix blank basemaps, failed imports, missing labels, or unprojected points.\n\n### Dense categorical grid\n\n- Use one compact horizontal selected-item summary, then a grid with exactly one\n  readable identifier per cell, then one small legend. Render only that\n  identifier as visible cell text; put all other metadata in an accessible label\n  or one summary line, not badges or fact grids. Allow only selection unless\n  asked.\n\n### Part-to-whole or time allocation\n\n- Use compact metrics and one stacked chart of category allocation per period.\n  Never substitute totals-only bars or duplicate it as a heatmap and totals\n  chart.\n\n## Layout and accessibility\n\n- Use semantic HTML, keyboard-accessible controls, and concise labels.\n- Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` for dynamic results, selections, and simulator\n  updates. Use `role=\"alert\"` for validation errors. Do not announce every\n  hover or animation frame.\n- Keep the top-level surface transparent and unframed, and fill the available\n  conversation width. Design for 736px, or 1,024px in wide mode, and support\n  widths down to 320px. Stack side-by-side content when it no longer fits.\n- At every supported width, text, controls, cards, toolbars, and dynamic content\n  must fit without overlap or clipping. Reflow by stacking or wrapping; use\n  `.table-responsive` only when table columns cannot fit. The host sizes the\n  frame to its content, so avoid fixed outer widths, other horizontal overflow,\n  internal scrolling, `position: fixed`, and viewport-height layouts.\n- Size every SVG from its actual container. At narrow widths, reduce ticks,\n  declutter annotations, and keep visible text at least 11 screen pixels;\n  never shrink a fixed-width `viewBox`.\n- Keep native tab order; never add `tabindex`.\n- Use native `button`, `input`, `select`, and `textarea` elements with matching\n  utilities; never recreate controls.\n- Keep browser or utility focus styles; never override them.\n- On coarse pointers, provide non-overlapping effective targets about 44px by\n  44px without breaking 320px layouts; visible icons and marks may stay small.\n  Keep fine-pointer controls compact, and let shared utilities own touch sizing\n  and at least 16px editable-field text.\n- Keep essential content and actions available without hover.\n\n## Typography\n\n- Scale type with `--font-size-base`. Use normal text by default and\n  `.text-small` only for secondary annotations (never below 11px).\n- `h1`, `h2`, and `h3` are available; use one concise visible heading for a\n  self-contained chart or graph, with short panel headings only when needed.\n  Do not restate the prompt or add a redundant title to other visualizations.\n- Use only weights `400` and `500`. Never set custom font sizes or line heights.\n- Use `.tabular-nums` on changing or aligned numbers. Avoid it for editorial or\n  decorative numerals.\n\n## Color\n\n- Make every fill, stroke, text, border, shadow, chart, and canvas color\n  theme-aware. Never hardcode light or dark palettes such as white panels,\n  off-white backgrounds, black text, slate strokes, or Tailwind color literals.\n- Keep text readable against its actual background. Muted or secondary colors\n  must retain clear contrast; never use `.text-muted` inside `.card` or another\n  filled container unless its background preserves that contrast.\n- Available theme variables include `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`,\n  `--card-foreground`, `--popover`, `--popover-foreground`, `--primary`,\n  `--primary-foreground`, `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--muted`,\n  `--muted-foreground`, `--accent`, `--accent-foreground`, `--destructive`,\n  `--border`, `--input`, `--ring`, `--blue`, `--orange`, `--green`, `--red`,\n  `--purple`, and `--yellow`. Use `currentColor` inside SVG.\n- Never add decorative borders, outlines, or strokes to progress tracks,\n  meters, bars, stacked segments, or other filled quantitative marks. Use a\n  subtle neutral or translucent track and distinguish marks with fill,\n  contrast, spacing, or opacity.\n- Use `--viz-series-1` for one measure or active state. Use `--viz-series-2`\n  through `--viz-series-6` only for important persistent category, series, or\n  status identity; never give every peer a different color by default.\n  - For categorical tiles or nodes, prefer a soft low-opacity series fill with a\n    neutral or transparent border; never color every outline.\n  - Keep mappings stable and pair color with labels, shapes, or line styles.\n  - Secondary series colors are theme-derived; never assume hues or use them\n    decoratively.\n- When color encodes a category or series, apply it consistently to the\n  corresponding visual marks—not just the legend—and keep large-area fills\n  subtle.\n- Use series colors only for chart lines, marks, and legend swatches. Keep\n  values, axis text, and direct labels in `--foreground` or\n  `--muted-foreground`.\n- Keep chart grids and inactive structure thin and neutral. Use 1-2px neutral\n  structural paths; never thicken, dash, or double-stroke the whole structure.\n- In each color pair, the base token is a surface and its\n  `-foreground` token is the content on that surface. Use `.btn-primary` for\n  high-emphasis actions; its neutral fill is supplied by the utility. Use\n  `--primary` and `--primary-foreground` for filled selected, active, or pressed\n  controls. Reserve `--accent` and `--accent-foreground` for subtle interactive\n  surfaces and soft highlights. Buttons with\n  `aria-pressed=\"true\"`, `aria-selected=\"true\"`, or `.is-selected` already use\n  the primary pairing.\n\n## Design system\n\n- Let utilities own geometry, appearance, and interaction. Use the matching\n  utility for every button and form control. Never restyle utilities,\n  descendants, or pseudo-elements: no custom sizes, spacing, borders, radii,\n  shadows, colors, or interaction states.\n\n### Surfaces and layout\n\n- `.card`: The only card-like HTML surface. Use its base class unchanged for a\n  necessary numeric summary, selected-item summary, or bounded interactive\n  field. Before adding a fill, border, radius, or shadow to any layout container,\n  either use `.card` or leave it transparent and unframed; never recreate card\n  chrome on rows, panels, tiles, sections, or wrappers. Keep charts, maps,\n  diagrams, tables, controls, and the whole visualization unframed. Never nest\n  cards; show 2-4 summaries near the top only when useful. Structural groupings\n  and repeated content are not bounded interactive fields. Organize them with\n  layout or visual marks, not container chrome.\n- `.viz-stat`: Use a summary `.card` with one muted label, one\n  `.viz-stat-value`, and at most one short context or delta line.\n- `.viz-grid`: Use for peer metrics or choices instead of a custom grid. It\n  creates as many equal-width columns as fit and stacks when narrow. Never use it\n  for the whole visual or a horizontally scrolling card row. Keep groups to 2-3\n  columns at 736px and controls in a separate row.\n- `.viz-row`: Use as a wrapping horizontal group with centered related values or\n  inline actions that may wrap when narrow.\n- `.progress` + `.progress-bar`:\n  `<div class=\"progress\" role=\"progressbar\" aria-label=\"Progress\" aria-valuenow=\"25\" aria-valuemin=\"0\" aria-valuemax=\"100\"><div class=\"progress-bar\" style=\"width:25%\"></div></div>`\n- `.viz-tile`: Add to a selectable dense-grid `.btn`; it stretches to fill its\n  grid cell, preserves category fill, and uses an accent ring instead of solid\n  selection. Never add another selected, pressed, border, outline, or shadow\n  rule.\n- `.viz-badge`: Use as a compact display-only accent pill for a short status,\n  category, or value; never as a button.\n- `.viz-controls`: Use as a wrapping row for controls affecting the same\n  visualization. Keep button groups compact. Put labeled fields directly inside\n  as `.form-label`; fields form at most two columns and stack when narrow.\n\n### Controls\n\n- `.btn`: Use for a content-sized secondary action. Add `.btn-primary` for one\n  main action per control group or `.btn-ghost` for low emphasis.\n- `.btn-block`: Add to a `.btn` only when the action should intentionally fill\n  the available inline space. Never use it for ordinary row actions.\n- `<a>`: Use for links. Add `.btn` to style a link as a button.\n- `[data-tooltip]`: Use for concise supplementary plain text on static or dynamic\n  triggers; the sandbox handles hover, focus, and touch and creates `.tooltip`\n  elements. Keep essential content visible and triggers labeled. Never use\n  `title`, custom markup, or initialization. Example:\n  `<button type=\"button\" data-tooltip=\"Reset view\">Reset</button>`.\n- `[data-tooltip-placement]`: Optionally prefer `top` (default), `right`,\n  `bottom`, or `left`; collision handling may flip it.\n- `.form-check`: Prefer a wrapping `<label class=\"form-check\">` around the\n  native `.form-check-input` and `.form-check-label` text so the whole row is\n  tappable. An explicit label with matching `for` and input `id` also works.\n- `.form-switch`: Add to `.form-check` around a native checkbox.\n- `.form-control`: Pair a native text, date, file, or color input—or a\n  textarea—with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-control-color`: Add to `.form-control` for a compact native color\n  input.\n- `.form-select`: Pair a native select with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-range`: Pair a native range with a visible label; put its current value\n  and units immediately before it.\n\n### Tables\n\n- `.table`: Use on a semantic table for a quiet, unframed data view. It provides\n  wrapping cells and subtle horizontal dividers without vertical gridlines. Use\n  sentence case for headers.\n- `.table-responsive`: Wrap a table when its columns cannot fit at narrow\n  widths. It contains horizontal overflow without clipping the visualization.\n- `.table-sm`: Add to `.table` when more rows need to fit; it reduces cell\n  padding without shrinking text.\n- `.text-end`, `.text-center`, and `.text-nowrap`: Use inside `.table` for\n  numeric/end alignment, centered values, or values that must stay on one line.\n  Numeric cells use tabular figures when end-aligned.\n\n### Text\n\n- `.text-small`: Use for the smallest host-scaled secondary chart labels and\n  annotations, never below 11px or for essential content.\n- `.text-muted`: Use for secondary units, captions, timestamps, and context,\n  never essential values or labels.\n- `.text-destructive`: Use only for error or validation text the user needs to\n  notice or act on.\n- `<code>`: Use for inline commands, file names, symbols, or short references;\n  put multiline code in `<pre><code>`.\n- `.sr-only`: Use for visually hidden accessible text.\n\n## Charts\n\n- Prefer inline SVG for simple charts and version-pinned approved-CDN\n  libraries when native interaction, scales, legends, or layout materially\n  improve the result.\n- Resolve theme colors before passing them to canvas or chart APIs that cannot\n  parse CSS variables or `light-dark(...)`; redraw when the theme changes.\n- Use a tooltip unless it would distract from a simple, directly labeled chart.\n  Keep chart-library tooltips and grouped legend interactions native; never\n  replace them with a custom one-point tooltip. For SVG, attach `data-tooltip`\n  directly to the real pointer-accessible mark and include its label, value,\n  and units; the sandbox handles themed positioning, keyboard focus, and touch.\n- Animate transitions between chart states so lines and marks move to their new\n  values, resampling paths when point counts differ. Do not animate initial\n  appearance or use fade-only effects; never loop motion, and honor\n  `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Scope SVG styles to the chart class. Never target every `svg` in a container\n  that also contains Lucide icons.\n- Include labeled axes, units, and directly labeled important values. Give every\n  chart, SVG, canvas, and widget a concise screen-reader summary using a role and\n  accessible name or description, SVG `<title>`/`<desc>`, fallback text, or an\n  `.sr-only` heading or description.\n- Reserve space for the longest formatted label at every supported width. Axis\n  ticks are secondary and may use `.text-small` when space is tight. Never\n  overlap or clip text against marks, axes, legends, labels, or edges; move or\n  reduce labels rather than squeeze them.\n- Add a legend only when multiple series cannot be labeled directly.\n- Pair color with shape or text so meaning never depends on color alone.\n\n## Icons and mockups\n\n- Use the sandbox-provided global `lucide`. Add an icon name with `data-lucide`:\n\n  ```html\n  <i data-lucide=\"search\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\n  ```\n\n- Lucide replaces the placeholder in place with an inline SVG. Icons are 16px\n  and inherit `currentColor`.\n- Mark decorative icons `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. Put action icons inside labeled\n  controls; use a visible label or `aria-label` for icon-only actions.\n- Let the sandbox initialize static icons after the fragment without blocking\n  first render. After adding icons dynamically, use\n  `lucide.createIcons({ attrs: { width: 16, height: 16 } })`.\n- Never load Lucide or another icon library from the network.\n- Use visibly labeled buttons and inputs for small interactions. Keep all\n  presentation-only interaction local to the fragment and make the first render\n  useful before input changes.\n- Use semantic controls, realistic spacing, and restrained chrome for mockups.\n  Never fake product screenshots when inspectable UI is needed.\n   691\tscope: Use for DATA-1/AUTH-1-style canonical-gateway issue delivery from clean `origin/dev` worktrees through bounded review, exact-head merge, and cleanup.\n   692\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=worktree, approval, and exact-head procedures are reusable, but code, PR, CI, and deployment evidence are snapshot-specific.\n   693\t\n   694\t## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees, success\n   695\t\n   696\t### rollout_summary_files\n   697\t\n   698\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, DATA-1 and AUTH-1 implemented, merged, and cleaned up)\n   699\t\n   700\t### keywords\n   701\t\n   702\t- DATA-1, AUTH-1, canonical-gateway, origin/dev, 72aabce, placement, CAS, HMAC assertions, replay protection, single-writer-strong, PR #121, PR #120, 1bf6f6bb\n   703\t\n   704\t## Task 2: Refetch and classify AUTH-1 PR review feedback once, success\n   705\t\n   706\t### rollout_summary_files\n   707\t\n   708\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, all 14 unresolved threads were stale or already fixed)\n   709\t\n   710\t### keywords\n   711\t\n   712\t- splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1, GH_CONFIG_DIR, reviewThreads, isOutdated, exact PR head, --match-head-commit, bf3a19d1, Node 22\n   713\t\n   714\t## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 then DATA-1 into dev, success\n   715\t\n   716\t### rollout_summary_files\n   717\t\n   718\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, exact-head merges completed and final origin/dev verified)\n   719\t\n   720\t### keywords\n   721\t\n   722\t- gh pr merge, --match-head-commit, origin/dev, git ort, 454c40f8, 3158fc83, e3b9834c, npm --workspace datafn/server test, git diff --check\n   723\t\n   724\t## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees, success\n   725\t\n   726\t### rollout_summary_files\n   727\t\n   728\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, dedicated worktrees removed without touching others)\n   729\t\n   730\t### keywords\n   731\t\n   732\t- git worktree remove, DATA-1, AUTH-1, process timeout, git worktree list, cleanup\n   733\t\n   734\t## User preferences\n   735\t\n   736\t- when the user requests new worktrees “off `origin/dev`” -> preserve the dirty checkout, create an isolated worktree from the authoritative exact ref, and keep requested scopes separate. [Task 1]\n   737\t- when the user requests end-to-end work in new worktrees -> preserve the dirty checkout, use freshly fetched `origin/dev` worktrees, and carry publication/verification through only when authorized. [Task 1]\n   738\t- when the user invokes `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> perform one bounded round: unresolved GraphQL threads first, then reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; validate against the exact worktree; push at most once; compare heads once; stop without post-push polling. [Task 2]\n   739\t\n   740\t## Reusable knowledge\n   741\t\n   742\t- DATA-1 PR #121 and AUTH-1 PR #120 were merged into `dev`; placement ownership remains separate from permission-grant projections/outboxes, while AUTH-1 keeps direct-mode compatibility alongside canonical gateway routing. [Task 1][Task 3]\n   743\t- DynamoDB placement uses `single-writer-strong` consistency and consistent reads; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic. Routing assertions bind identity plus relevant method/path/body data; stale routing may retry only once before execution. [Task 1]\n   744\t- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`. Review every unresolved thread against `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head; timestamp-only comment queries are insufficient. [Task 2]\n   745\t- Exact merge pattern: merge the first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch resulting `origin/dev`, merge it into the dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, reread PR head, merge with an exact-head guard, then verify ancestry in `origin/dev`. DATA-1 validation: 92 files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 3]\n   746\t\n   747\t## Failures and how to do differently\n   748\t\nthread_id: 01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af\nupdated_at: 2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00\nrollout_path: /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl\ncwd: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\ngit_branch: SFNS-2\n\n# DATA-1 and AUTH-1 were implemented, reviewed, merged, and their dedicated worktrees removed\n\nRollout context: The user requested end-to-end work on Linear issues DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in separate worktrees based on freshly fetched `origin/dev`, followed by PR review remediation, merging, and cleanup. The original dirty checkout `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev` was preserved. Hosted Skillplane workflow `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1` was retrieved and followed.\n\n## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user asked to “work DATA-1 and AUTH-1 issues in a new worktrees off of origin/dev end to end” -> preserve the existing checkout, use separate clean worktrees from the authoritative fetched ref, and keep issue scopes isolated.\n- The workflow required local implementation and verification before publication approval, then exact issue-ID branches for publication.\n\nKey steps:\n- Read both complete Linear issues, all comments, linked architecture/rationale documents, relations, and project metadata.\n- Fetched `origin/dev` at `72aabce915426325450663feffd7a53089ef4489` and created detached worktrees under `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1` and `AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 implemented tenant placement separate from permission indexing, atomic placement/CAS epoch moves, gateway dispatch, signed assertions, pre-execution fencing, bounded retry, sync/WebSocket behavior, migration fencing, observability, provider-neutral adapters, tests, and documentation.\n- AUTH-1 implemented canonical gateway routing with direct-mode compatibility, identity placement/CAS, signed HMAC assertions and replay protection, pre-side-effect routing, TypeScript/Python adapter support, OAuth/session semantics, deletion fencing, migration behavior, Swift/client updates, tests, and documentation.\n- DATA-1 was published as PR #121 and AUTH-1 as PR #120. DATA-1 later received one narrow remediation fixing placement validation order before custom-route middleware; commit `1bf6f6bb` was pushed and verified against the PR head.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- The original checkout was dirty with unrelated `.conduct` artifacts; do not clean or modify it during similar work.\n- AUTH-1’s initial remote CI had unrelated repository failures, including a FastAPI `StopIteration` issue in prior history; distinguish local feature validation from repository-wide CI and live multi-region proof.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Placement ownership must remain a separate source of truth from permission-grant projections/outboxes.\n- Routing assertions should bind request identity and relevant method/path/body data, and stale routing may retry only once before execution.\n- DynamoDB placement used `single-writer-strong` consistency; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic.\n\nReferences:\n- Hosted workflow: `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1`, digest prefix `716e55…abaf`.\n- Worktrees: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`, `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 PR #121: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/121`.\n- AUTH-1 PR #120: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/120`.\n\n## Task 2: Bounded AUTH-1 PR review remediation\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user invoked `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> use one bounded review round: fetch unresolved GraphQL threads plus reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; classify against the exact PR worktree; push at most once; verify heads once; do not refetch or poll afterward.\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 worktree was clean and local, remote, and PR heads matched `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- Frozen review snapshot contained 56 threads, 14 unresolved, 10 with current comments, 4 outdated-only threads, 49 reviews, 4 issue comments, 54 inline comments, and 28 checks.\n- Verified all unresolved findings against current code. Middleware execution, `/runtime` compatibility/global classification, cell-region registration precedence, deletion fencing, error propagation, and related issues were already fixed or stale. No edit, commit, or push was needed.\n- Recorded unrelated failed CI causes: DevFn required Node 22 but CI used Node 20; BillFn and SQLAlchemy had pre-existing typing issues; python-core had Ruff import formatting failures.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Do not treat every unresolved GitHub thread as actionable; inspect `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head.\n- Do not use timestamp-only comment queries; unresolved historical threads can remain actionable, while many current-looking threads are stale.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`.\n- Use exact-head guards such as `--match-head-commit`; after an authorized push, compare local, remote, and live PR SHAs once and stop.\n\nReferences:\n- Skill: `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md`.\n- AUTH-1 final reviewed head: `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- AUTH-1 PR review result: 0 fixes; all 14 unresolved threads stale/already fixed.\n\n## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 and DATA-1 into dev\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed both PRs were clean and mergeable; merge commits were enabled.\n- Merged AUTH-1 first with an exact-head guard. PR #120 merged at `REDACTED`.\n- Fetched the resulting `origin/dev` and merged it into DATA-1. Git’s `ort` merge completed without conflicts; no manual conflict resolution was required.\n- Ran the combined DATA-1 server suite: 92 test files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; DataFn typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Pushed DATA-1 merge commit `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`, verified local/remote/PR heads, then merged PR #121 at `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Verified both PR heads are ancestors of final `origin/dev` `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Refresh a dependent PR against the actual post-merge `origin/dev` before merging it. This avoided guessing about overlap and confirmed whether conflicts existed.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Exact merge pattern: inspect PR state/heads, merge first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch `origin/dev`, merge into dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, re-read PR head, merge with exact-head guard, and verify ancestry in `origin/dev`.\n\nReferences:\n- AUTH-1 merge commit: `REDACTED`.\n- DATA-1 branch merge commit before PR merge: `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`.\n- Final `origin/dev`: `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Validation: `npm --workspace datafn/server test`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run typecheck`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run build`, `git diff --check`.\n\n## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 and DATA-1 worktrees were clean and registered.\n- Ran Git worktree removal for both paths. The first command exceeded its execution window, but the process was checked and allowed to finish rather than racing a second cleanup.\n- Verified both directories were absent, Git no longer listed them, and local branches remained available: AUTH-1 at `bf3a19d1…`, DATA-1 at `3158fc83…`.\n- Other worktrees were untouched.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- `git worktree remove` may take longer than the command timeout on large repositories. Check for an active process before retrying.\n\nReferences:\n- Removed: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1` and `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`.\n- Remaining branches: `AUTH-1`, `DATA-1`.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1833},"formatted_output":"     500 /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\n  small multiples. Render every requested dimension simultaneously; never hide\n  one behind a toggle.\n\n### Maps\n\n- Let the map dominate the composition. Use at most one compact\n  selection/detail area and only requested controls.\n- Always project published GeoJSON/TopoJSON and sourced longitude/latitude with\n  `d3-geo`; never hard-code or hand-draw geographic outlines. Use schematic maps\n  only when asked.\n- For world countries, import\n  `https://esm.sh/@d3-maps/atlas@1.0.0/world/countries/countries-110m` and convert\n  it with `topojson-client@3.1.0` using\n  `feature(world, world.objects.features).features`. Join input ISO3 directly to\n  `feature.properties.id`, which is already ISO3; do not convert it to numbers.\n- For US states or counties, use\n  `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/us-atlas@3/counties-10m.json/+esm`. For ZIP/ZCTA\n  or city boundaries, download official Census or local open-data GeoJSON; do\n  not guess sibling atlas paths or import raw JSON as JavaScript.\n- Keep maps geographically legible: for local points, fetch published\n  neighborhood, street, or comparable geometry; a blank field or lone\n  administrative outline is not a basemap. Show the full city or region behind\n  points or partial choropleths, and frame the locations with modest padding.\n- Include the verified geometry in the final HTML. Open it before replying and\n  fix blank basemaps, failed imports, missing labels, or unprojected points.\n\n### Dense categorical grid\n\n- Use one compact horizontal selected-item summary, then a grid with exactly one\n  readable identifier per cell, then one small legend. Render only that\n  identifier as visible cell text; put all other metadata in an accessible label\n  or one summary line, not badges or fact grids. Allow only selection unless\n  asked.\n\n### Part-to-whole or time allocation\n\n- Use compact metrics and one stacked chart of category allocation per period.\n  Never substitute totals-only bars or duplicate it as a heatmap and totals\n  chart.\n\n## Layout and accessibility\n\n- Use semantic HTML, keyboard-accessible controls, and concise labels.\n- Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` for dynamic results, selections, and simulator\n  updates. Use `role=\"alert\"` for validation errors. Do not announce every\n  hover or animation frame.\n- Keep the top-level surface transparent and unframed, and fill the available\n  conversation width. Design for 736px, or 1,024px in wide mode, and support\n  widths down to 320px. Stack side-by-side content when it no longer fits.\n- At every supported width, text, controls, cards, toolbars, and dynamic content\n  must fit without overlap or clipping. Reflow by stacking or wrapping; use\n  `.table-responsive` only when table columns cannot fit. The host sizes the\n  frame to its content, so avoid fixed outer widths, other horizontal overflow,\n  internal scrolling, `position: fixed`, and viewport-height layouts.\n- Size every SVG from its actual container. At narrow widths, reduce ticks,\n  declutter annotations, and keep visible text at least 11 screen pixels;\n  never shrink a fixed-width `viewBox`.\n- Keep native tab order; never add `tabindex`.\n- Use native `button`, `input`, `select`, and `textarea` elements with matching\n  utilities; never recreate controls.\n- Keep browser or utility focus styles; never override them.\n- On coarse pointers, provide non-overlapping effective targets about 44px by\n  44px without breaking 320px layouts; visible icons and marks may stay small.\n  Keep fine-pointer controls compact, and let shared utilities own touch sizing\n  and at least 16px editable-field text.\n- Keep essential content and actions available without hover.\n\n## Typography\n\n- Scale type with `--font-size-base`. Use normal text by default and\n  `.text-small` only for secondary annotations (never below 11px).\n- `h1`, `h2`, and `h3` are available; use one concise visible heading for a\n  self-contained chart or graph, with short panel headings only when needed.\n  Do not restate the prompt or add a redundant title to other visualizations.\n- Use only weights `400` and `500`. Never set custom font sizes or line heights.\n- Use `.tabular-nums` on changing or aligned numbers. Avoid it for editorial or\n  decorative numerals.\n\n## Color\n\n- Make every fill, stroke, text, border, shadow, chart, and canvas color\n  theme-aware. Never hardcode light or dark palettes such as white panels,\n  off-white backgrounds, black text, slate strokes, or Tailwind color literals.\n- Keep text readable against its actual background. Muted or secondary colors\n  must retain clear contrast; never use `.text-muted` inside `.card` or another\n  filled container unless its background preserves that contrast.\n- Available theme variables include `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`,\n  `--card-foreground`, `--popover`, `--popover-foreground`, `--primary`,\n  `--primary-foreground`, `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--muted`,\n  `--muted-foreground`, `--accent`, `--accent-foreground`, `--destructive`,\n  `--border`, `--input`, `--ring`, `--blue`, `--orange`, `--green`, `--red`,\n  `--purple`, and `--yellow`. Use `currentColor` inside SVG.\n- Never add decorative borders, outlines, or strokes to progress tracks,\n  meters, bars, stacked segments, or other filled quantitative marks. Use a\n  subtle neutral or translucent track and distinguish marks with fill,\n  contrast, spacing, or opacity.\n- Use `--viz-series-1` for one measure or active state. Use `--viz-series-2`\n  through `--viz-series-6` only for important persistent category, series, or\n  status identity; never give every peer a different color by default.\n  - For categorical tiles or nodes, prefer a soft low-opacity series fill with a\n    neutral or transparent border; never color every outline.\n  - Keep mappings stable and pair color with labels, shapes, or line styles.\n  - Secondary series colors are theme-derived; never assume hues or use them\n    decoratively.\n- When color encodes a category or series, apply it consistently to the\n  corresponding visual marks—not just the legend—and keep large-area fills\n  subtle.\n- Use series colors only for chart lines, marks, and legend swatches. Keep\n  values, axis text, and direct labels in `--foreground` or\n  `--muted-foreground`.\n- Keep chart grids and inactive structure thin and neutral. Use 1-2px neutral\n  structural paths; never thicken, dash, or double-stroke the whole structure.\n- In each color pair, the base token is a surface and its\n  `-foreground` token is the content on that surface. Use `.btn-primary` for\n  high-emphasis actions; its neutral fill is supplied by the utility. Use\n  `--primary` and `--primary-foreground` for filled selected, active, or pressed\n  controls. Reserve `--accent` and `--accent-foreground` for subtle interactive\n  surfaces and soft highlights. Buttons with\n  `aria-pressed=\"true\"`, `aria-selected=\"true\"`, or `.is-selected` already use\n  the primary pairing.\n\n## Design system\n\n- Let utilities own geometry, appearance, and interaction. Use the matching\n  utility for every button and form control. Never restyle utilities,\n  descendants, or pseudo-elements: no custom sizes, spacing, borders, radii,\n  shadows, colors, or interaction states.\n\n### Surfaces and layout\n\n- `.card`: The only card-like HTML surface. Use its base class unchanged for a\n  necessary numeric summary, selected-item summary, or bounded interactive\n  field. Before adding a fill, border, radius, or shadow to any layout container,\n  either use `.card` or leave it transparent and unframed; never recreate card\n  chrome on rows, panels, tiles, sections, or wrappers. Keep charts, maps,\n  diagrams, tables, controls, and the whole visualization unframed. Never nest\n  cards; show 2-4 summaries near the top only when useful. Structural groupings\n  and repeated content are not bounded interactive fields. Organize them with\n  layout or visual marks, not container chrome.\n- `.viz-stat`: Use a summary `.card` with one muted label, one\n  `.viz-stat-value`, and at most one short context or delta line.\n- `.viz-grid`: Use for peer metrics or choices instead of a custom grid. It\n  creates as many equal-width columns as fit and stacks when narrow. Never use it\n  for the whole visual or a horizontally scrolling card row. Keep groups to 2-3\n  columns at 736px and controls in a separate row.\n- `.viz-row`: Use as a wrapping horizontal group with centered related values or\n  inline actions that may wrap when narrow.\n- `.progress` + `.progress-bar`:\n  `<div class=\"progress\" role=\"progressbar\" aria-label=\"Progress\" aria-valuenow=\"25\" aria-valuemin=\"0\" aria-valuemax=\"100\"><div class=\"progress-bar\" style=\"width:25%\"></div></div>`\n- `.viz-tile`: Add to a selectable dense-grid `.btn`; it stretches to fill its\n  grid cell, preserves category fill, and uses an accent ring instead of solid\n  selection. Never add another selected, pressed, border, outline, or shadow\n  rule.\n- `.viz-badge`: Use as a compact display-only accent pill for a short status,\n  category, or value; never as a button.\n- `.viz-controls`: Use as a wrapping row for controls affecting the same\n  visualization. Keep button groups compact. Put labeled fields directly inside\n  as `.form-label`; fields form at most two columns and stack when narrow.\n\n### Controls\n\n- `.btn`: Use for a content-sized secondary action. Add `.btn-primary` for one\n  main action per control group or `.btn-ghost` for low emphasis.\n- `.btn-block`: Add to a `.btn` only when the action should intentionally fill\n  the available inline space. Never use it for ordinary row actions.\n- `<a>`: Use for links. Add `.btn` to style a link as a button.\n- `[data-tooltip]`: Use for concise supplementary plain text on static or dynamic\n  triggers; the sandbox handles hover, focus, and touch and creates `.tooltip`\n  elements. Keep essential content visible and triggers labeled. Never use\n  `title`, custom markup, or initialization. Example:\n  `<button type=\"button\" data-tooltip=\"Reset view\">Reset</button>`.\n- `[data-tooltip-placement]`: Optionally prefer `top` (default), `right`,\n  `bottom`, or `left`; collision handling may flip it.\n- `.form-check`: Prefer a wrapping `<label class=\"form-check\">` around the\n  native `.form-check-input` and `.form-check-label` text so the whole row is\n  tappable. An explicit label with matching `for` and input `id` also works.\n- `.form-switch`: Add to `.form-check` around a native checkbox.\n- `.form-control`: Pair a native text, date, file, or color input—or a\n  textarea—with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-control-color`: Add to `.form-control` for a compact native color\n  input.\n- `.form-select`: Pair a native select with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-range`: Pair a native range with a visible label; put its current value\n  and units immediately before it.\n\n### Tables\n\n- `.table`: Use on a semantic table for a quiet, unframed data view. It provides\n  wrapping cells and subtle horizontal dividers without vertical gridlines. Use\n  sentence case for headers.\n- `.table-responsive`: Wrap a table when its columns cannot fit at narrow\n  widths. It contains horizontal overflow without clipping the visualization.\n- `.table-sm`: Add to `.table` when more rows need to fit; it reduces cell\n  padding without shrinking text.\n- `.text-end`, `.text-center`, and `.text-nowrap`: Use inside `.table` for\n  numeric/end alignment, centered values, or values that must stay on one line.\n  Numeric cells use tabular figures when end-aligned.\n\n### Text\n\n- `.text-small`: Use for the smallest host-scaled secondary chart labels and\n  annotations, never below 11px or for essential content.\n- `.text-muted`: Use for secondary units, captions, timestamps, and context,\n  never essential values or labels.\n- `.text-destructive`: Use only for error or validation text the user needs to\n  notice or act on.\n- `<code>`: Use for inline commands, file names, symbols, or short references;\n  put multiline code in `<pre><code>`.\n- `.sr-only`: Use for visually hidden accessible text.\n\n## Charts\n\n- Prefer inline SVG for simple charts and version-pinned approved-CDN\n  libraries when native interaction, scales, legends, or layout materially\n  improve the result.\n- Resolve theme colors before passing them to canvas or chart APIs that cannot\n  parse CSS variables or `light-dark(...)`; redraw when the theme changes.\n- Use a tooltip unless it would distract from a simple, directly labeled chart.\n  Keep chart-library tooltips and grouped legend interactions native; never\n  replace them with a custom one-point tooltip. For SVG, attach `data-tooltip`\n  directly to the real pointer-accessible mark and include its label, value,\n  and units; the sandbox handles themed positioning, keyboard focus, and touch.\n- Animate transitions between chart states so lines and marks move to their new\n  values, resampling paths when point counts differ. Do not animate initial\n  appearance or use fade-only effects; never loop motion, and honor\n  `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Scope SVG styles to the chart class. Never target every `svg` in a container\n  that also contains Lucide icons.\n- Include labeled axes, units, and directly labeled important values. Give every\n  chart, SVG, canvas, and widget a concise screen-reader summary using a role and\n  accessible name or description, SVG `<title>`/`<desc>`, fallback text, or an\n  `.sr-only` heading or description.\n- Reserve space for the longest formatted label at every supported width. Axis\n  ticks are secondary and may use `.text-small` when space is tight. Never\n  overlap or clip text against marks, axes, legends, labels, or edges; move or\n  reduce labels rather than squeeze them.\n- Add a legend only when multiple series cannot be labeled directly.\n- Pair color with shape or text so meaning never depends on color alone.\n\n## Icons and mockups\n\n- Use the sandbox-provided global `lucide`. Add an icon name with `data-lucide`:\n\n  ```html\n  <i data-lucide=\"search\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\n  ```\n\n- Lucide replaces the placeholder in place with an inline SVG. Icons are 16px\n  and inherit `currentColor`.\n- Mark decorative icons `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. Put action icons inside labeled\n  controls; use a visible label or `aria-label` for icon-only actions.\n- Let the sandbox initialize static icons after the fragment without blocking\n  first render. After adding icons dynamically, use\n  `lucide.createIcons({ attrs: { width: 16, height: 16 } })`.\n- Never load Lucide or another icon library from the network.\n- Use visibly labeled buttons and inputs for small interactions. Keep all\n  presentation-only interaction local to the fragment and make the first render\n  useful before input changes.\n- Use semantic controls, realistic spacing, and restrained chrome for mockups.\n  Never fake product screenshots when inspectable UI is needed.\n   691\tscope: Use for DATA-1/AUTH-1-style canonical-gateway issue delivery from clean `origin/dev` worktrees through bounded review, exact-head merge, and cleanup.\n   692\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=worktree, approval, and exact-head procedures are reusable, but code, PR, CI, and deployment evidence are snapshot-specific.\n   693\t\n   694\t## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees, success\n   695\t\n   696\t### rollout_summary_files\n   697\t\n   698\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, DATA-1 and AUTH-1 implemented, merged, and cleaned up)\n   699\t\n   700\t### keywords\n   701\t\n   702\t- DATA-1, AUTH-1, canonical-gateway, origin/dev, 72aabce, placement, CAS, HMAC assertions, replay protection, single-writer-strong, PR #121, PR #120, 1bf6f6bb\n   703\t\n   704\t## Task 2: Refetch and classify AUTH-1 PR review feedback once, success\n   705\t\n   706\t### rollout_summary_files\n   707\t\n   708\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, all 14 unresolved threads were stale or already fixed)\n   709\t\n   710\t### keywords\n   711\t\n   712\t- splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1, GH_CONFIG_DIR, reviewThreads, isOutdated, exact PR head, --match-head-commit, bf3a19d1, Node 22\n   713\t\n   714\t## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 then DATA-1 into dev, success\n   715\t\n   716\t### rollout_summary_files\n   717\t\n   718\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, exact-head merges completed and final origin/dev verified)\n   719\t\n   720\t### keywords\n   721\t\n   722\t- gh pr merge, --match-head-commit, origin/dev, git ort, 454c40f8, 3158fc83, e3b9834c, npm --workspace datafn/server test, git diff --check\n   723\t\n   724\t## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees, success\n   725\t\n   726\t### rollout_summary_files\n   727\t\n   728\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, dedicated worktrees removed without touching others)\n   729\t\n   730\t### keywords\n   731\t\n   732\t- git worktree remove, DATA-1, AUTH-1, process timeout, git worktree list, cleanup\n   733\t\n   734\t## User preferences\n   735\t\n   736\t- when the user requests new worktrees “off `origin/dev`” -> preserve the dirty checkout, create an isolated worktree from the authoritative exact ref, and keep requested scopes separate. [Task 1]\n   737\t- when the user requests end-to-end work in new worktrees -> preserve the dirty checkout, use freshly fetched `origin/dev` worktrees, and carry publication/verification through only when authorized. [Task 1]\n   738\t- when the user invokes `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> perform one bounded round: unresolved GraphQL threads first, then reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; validate against the exact worktree; push at most once; compare heads once; stop without post-push polling. [Task 2]\n   739\t\n   740\t## Reusable knowledge\n   741\t\n   742\t- DATA-1 PR #121 and AUTH-1 PR #120 were merged into `dev`; placement ownership remains separate from permission-grant projections/outboxes, while AUTH-1 keeps direct-mode compatibility alongside canonical gateway routing. [Task 1][Task 3]\n   743\t- DynamoDB placement uses `single-writer-strong` consistency and consistent reads; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic. Routing assertions bind identity plus relevant method/path/body data; stale routing may retry only once before execution. [Task 1]\n   744\t- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`. Review every unresolved thread against `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head; timestamp-only comment queries are insufficient. [Task 2]\n   745\t- Exact merge pattern: merge the first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch resulting `origin/dev`, merge it into the dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, reread PR head, merge with an exact-head guard, then verify ancestry in `origin/dev`. DATA-1 validation: 92 files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 3]\n   746\t\n   747\t## Failures and how to do differently\n   748\t\nthread_id: 01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af\nupdated_at: 2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00\nrollout_path: /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl\ncwd: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\ngit_branch: SFNS-2\n\n# DATA-1 and AUTH-1 were implemented, reviewed, merged, and their dedicated worktrees removed\n\nRollout context: The user requested end-to-end work on Linear issues DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in separate worktrees based on freshly fetched `origin/dev`, followed by PR review remediation, merging, and cleanup. The original dirty checkout `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev` was preserved. Hosted Skillplane workflow `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1` was retrieved and followed.\n\n## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user asked to “work DATA-1 and AUTH-1 issues in a new worktrees off of origin/dev end to end” -> preserve the existing checkout, use separate clean worktrees from the authoritative fetched ref, and keep issue scopes isolated.\n- The workflow required local implementation and verification before publication approval, then exact issue-ID branches for publication.\n\nKey steps:\n- Read both complete Linear issues, all comments, linked architecture/rationale documents, relations, and project metadata.\n- Fetched `origin/dev` at `72aabce915426325450663feffd7a53089ef4489` and created detached worktrees under `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1` and `AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 implemented tenant placement separate from permission indexing, atomic placement/CAS epoch moves, gateway dispatch, signed assertions, pre-execution fencing, bounded retry, sync/WebSocket behavior, migration fencing, observability, provider-neutral adapters, tests, and documentation.\n- AUTH-1 implemented canonical gateway routing with direct-mode compatibility, identity placement/CAS, signed HMAC assertions and replay protection, pre-side-effect routing, TypeScript/Python adapter support, OAuth/session semantics, deletion fencing, migration behavior, Swift/client updates, tests, and documentation.\n- DATA-1 was published as PR #121 and AUTH-1 as PR #120. DATA-1 later received one narrow remediation fixing placement validation order before custom-route middleware; commit `1bf6f6bb` was pushed and verified against the PR head.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- The original checkout was dirty with unrelated `.conduct` artifacts; do not clean or modify it during similar work.\n- AUTH-1’s initial remote CI had unrelated repository failures, including a FastAPI `StopIteration` issue in prior history; distinguish local feature validation from repository-wide CI and live multi-region proof.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Placement ownership must remain a separate source of truth from permission-grant projections/outboxes.\n- Routing assertions should bind request identity and relevant method/path/body data, and stale routing may retry only once before execution.\n- DynamoDB placement used `single-writer-strong` consistency; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic.\n\nReferences:\n- Hosted workflow: `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1`, digest prefix `716e55…abaf`.\n- Worktrees: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`, `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 PR #121: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/121`.\n- AUTH-1 PR #120: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/120`.\n\n## Task 2: Bounded AUTH-1 PR review remediation\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user invoked `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> use one bounded review round: fetch unresolved GraphQL threads plus reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; classify against the exact PR worktree; push at most once; verify heads once; do not refetch or poll afterward.\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 worktree was clean and local, remote, and PR heads matched `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- Frozen review snapshot contained 56 threads, 14 unresolved, 10 with current comments, 4 outdated-only threads, 49 reviews, 4 issue comments, 54 inline comments, and 28 checks.\n- Verified all unresolved findings against current code. Middleware execution, `/runtime` compatibility/global classification, cell-region registration precedence, deletion fencing, error propagation, and related issues were already fixed or stale. No edit, commit, or push was needed.\n- Recorded unrelated failed CI causes: DevFn required Node 22 but CI used Node 20; BillFn and SQLAlchemy had pre-existing typing issues; python-core had Ruff import formatting failures.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Do not treat every unresolved GitHub thread as actionable; inspect `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head.\n- Do not use timestamp-only comment queries; unresolved historical threads can remain actionable, while many current-looking threads are stale.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`.\n- Use exact-head guards such as `--match-head-commit`; after an authorized push, compare local, remote, and live PR SHAs once and stop.\n\nReferences:\n- Skill: `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md`.\n- AUTH-1 final reviewed head: `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- AUTH-1 PR review result: 0 fixes; all 14 unresolved threads stale/already fixed.\n\n## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 and DATA-1 into dev\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed both PRs were clean and mergeable; merge commits were enabled.\n- Merged AUTH-1 first with an exact-head guard. PR #120 merged at `REDACTED`.\n- Fetched the resulting `origin/dev` and merged it into DATA-1. Git’s `ort` merge completed without conflicts; no manual conflict resolution was required.\n- Ran the combined DATA-1 server suite: 92 test files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; DataFn typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Pushed DATA-1 merge commit `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`, verified local/remote/PR heads, then merged PR #121 at `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Verified both PR heads are ancestors of final `origin/dev` `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Refresh a dependent PR against the actual post-merge `origin/dev` before merging it. This avoided guessing about overlap and confirmed whether conflicts existed.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Exact merge pattern: inspect PR state/heads, merge first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch `origin/dev`, merge into dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, re-read PR head, merge with exact-head guard, and verify ancestry in `origin/dev`.\n\nReferences:\n- AUTH-1 merge commit: `REDACTED`.\n- DATA-1 branch merge commit before PR merge: `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`.\n- Final `origin/dev`: `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Validation: `npm --workspace datafn/server test`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run typecheck`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run build`, `git diff --check`.\n\n## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 and DATA-1 worktrees were clean and registered.\n- Ran Git worktree removal for both paths. The first command exceeded its execution window, but the process was checked and allowed to finish rather than racing a second cleanup.\n- Verified both directories were absent, Git no longer listed them, and local branches remained available: AUTH-1 at `bf3a19d1…`, DATA-1 at `3158fc83…`.\n- Other worktrees were untouched.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- `git worktree remove` may take longer than the command timeout on large repositories. Check for an active process before retrying.\n\nReferences:\n- Removed: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1` and `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`.\n- Remaining branches: `AUTH-1`, `DATA-1`.\n\n"},"started_at_ms":1787984670044,"completed_at_ms":1787984670045}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:30.051Z","ordinal":12107,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04c30-ed63-7292-8a17-cd63517e2830","call_id":"call_Id0CQqMmsdDwy696ABaFCywO","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"     500 /Users/serro/.codex/plugins/cache/openai-bundled/visualize/1.0.23/skills/visualize/SKILL.md\n  small multiples. Render every requested dimension simultaneously; never hide\n  one behind a toggle.\n\n### Maps\n\n- Let the map dominate the composition. Use at most one compact\n  selection/detail area and only requested controls.\n- Always project published GeoJSON/TopoJSON and sourced longitude/latitude with\n  `d3-geo`; never hard-code or hand-draw geographic outlines. Use schematic maps\n  only when asked.\n- For world countries, import\n  `https://esm.sh/@d3-maps/atlas@1.0.0/world/countries/countries-110m` and convert\n  it with `topojson-client@3.1.0` using\n  `feature(world, world.objects.features).features`. Join input ISO3 directly to\n  `feature.properties.id`, which is already ISO3; do not convert it to numbers.\n- For US states or counties, use\n  `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/us-atlas@3/counties-10m.json/+esm`. For ZIP/ZCTA\n  or city boundaries, download official Census or local open-data GeoJSON; do\n  not guess sibling atlas paths or import raw JSON as JavaScript.\n- Keep maps geographically legible: for local points, fetch published\n  neighborhood, street, or comparable geometry; a blank field or lone\n  administrative outline is not a basemap. Show the full city or region behind\n  points or partial choropleths, and frame the locations with modest padding.\n- Include the verified geometry in the final HTML. Open it before replying and\n  fix blank basemaps, failed imports, missing labels, or unprojected points.\n\n### Dense categorical grid\n\n- Use one compact horizontal selected-item summary, then a grid with exactly one\n  readable identifier per cell, then one small legend. Render only that\n  identifier as visible cell text; put all other metadata in an accessible label\n  or one summary line, not badges or fact grids. Allow only selection unless\n  asked.\n\n### Part-to-whole or time allocation\n\n- Use compact metrics and one stacked chart of category allocation per period.\n  Never substitute totals-only bars or duplicate it as a heatmap and totals\n  chart.\n\n## Layout and accessibility\n\n- Use semantic HTML, keyboard-accessible controls, and concise labels.\n- Use `aria-live=\"polite\"` for dynamic results, selections, and simulator\n  updates. Use `role=\"alert\"` for validation errors. Do not announce every\n  hover or animation frame.\n- Keep the top-level surface transparent and unframed, and fill the available\n  conversation width. Design for 736px, or 1,024px in wide mode, and support\n  widths down to 320px. Stack side-by-side content when it no longer fits.\n- At every supported width, text, controls, cards, toolbars, and dynamic content\n  must fit without overlap or clipping. Reflow by stacking or wrapping; use\n  `.table-responsive` only when table columns cannot fit. The host sizes the\n  frame to its content, so avoid fixed outer widths, other horizontal overflow,\n  internal scrolling, `position: fixed`, and viewport-height layouts.\n- Size every SVG from its actual container. At narrow widths, reduce ticks,\n  declutter annotations, and keep visible text at least 11 screen pixels;\n  never shrink a fixed-width `viewBox`.\n- Keep native tab order; never add `tabindex`.\n- Use native `button`, `input`, `select`, and `textarea` elements with matching\n  utilities; never recreate controls.\n- Keep browser or utility focus styles; never override them.\n- On coarse pointers, provide non-overlapping effective targets about 44px by\n  44px without breaking 320px layouts; visible icons and marks may stay small.\n  Keep fine-pointer controls compact, and let shared utilities own touch sizing\n  and at least 16px editable-field text.\n- Keep essential content and actions available without hover.\n\n## Typography\n\n- Scale type with `--font-size-base`. Use normal text by default and\n  `.text-small` only for secondary annotations (never below 11px).\n- `h1`, `h2`, and `h3` are available; use one concise visible heading for a\n  self-contained chart or graph, with short panel headings only when needed.\n  Do not restate the prompt or add a redundant title to other visualizations.\n- Use only weights `400` and `500`. Never set custom font sizes or line heights.\n- Use `.tabular-nums` on changing or aligned numbers. Avoid it for editorial or\n  decorative numerals.\n\n## Color\n\n- Make every fill, stroke, text, border, shadow, chart, and canvas color\n  theme-aware. Never hardcode light or dark palettes such as white panels,\n  off-white backgrounds, black text, slate strokes, or Tailwind color literals.\n- Keep text readable against its actual background. Muted or secondary colors\n  must retain clear contrast; never use `.text-muted` inside `.card` or another\n  filled container unless its background preserves that contrast.\n- Available theme variables include `--background`, `--foreground`, `--card`,\n  `--card-foreground`, `--popover`, `--popover-foreground`, `--primary`,\n  `--primary-foreground`, `--secondary`, `--secondary-foreground`, `--muted`,\n  `--muted-foreground`, `--accent`, `--accent-foreground`, `--destructive`,\n  `--border`, `--input`, `--ring`, `--blue`, `--orange`, `--green`, `--red`,\n  `--purple`, and `--yellow`. Use `currentColor` inside SVG.\n- Never add decorative borders, outlines, or strokes to progress tracks,\n  meters, bars, stacked segments, or other filled quantitative marks. Use a\n  subtle neutral or translucent track and distinguish marks with fill,\n  contrast, spacing, or opacity.\n- Use `--viz-series-1` for one measure or active state. Use `--viz-series-2`\n  through `--viz-series-6` only for important persistent category, series, or\n  status identity; never give every peer a different color by default.\n  - For categorical tiles or nodes, prefer a soft low-opacity series fill with a\n    neutral or transparent border; never color every outline.\n  - Keep mappings stable and pair color with labels, shapes, or line styles.\n  - Secondary series colors are theme-derived; never assume hues or use them\n    decoratively.\n- When color encodes a category or series, apply it consistently to the\n  corresponding visual marks—not just the legend—and keep large-area fills\n  subtle.\n- Use series colors only for chart lines, marks, and legend swatches. Keep\n  values, axis text, and direct labels in `--foreground` or\n  `--muted-foreground`.\n- Keep chart grids and inactive structure thin and neutral. Use 1-2px neutral\n  structural paths; never thicken, dash, or double-stroke the whole structure.\n- In each color pair, the base token is a surface and its\n  `-foreground` token is the content on that surface. Use `.btn-primary` for\n  high-emphasis actions; its neutral fill is supplied by the utility. Use\n  `--primary` and `--primary-foreground` for filled selected, active, or pressed\n  controls. Reserve `--accent` and `--accent-foreground` for subtle interactive\n  surfaces and soft highlights. Buttons with\n  `aria-pressed=\"true\"`, `aria-selected=\"true\"`, or `.is-selected` already use\n  the primary pairing.\n\n## Design system\n\n- Let utilities own geometry, appearance, and interaction. Use the matching\n  utility for every button and form control. Never restyle utilities,\n  descendants, or pseudo-elements: no custom sizes, spacing, borders, radii,\n  shadows, colors, or interaction states.\n\n### Surfaces and layout\n\n- `.card`: The only card-like HTML surface. Use its base class unchanged for a\n  necessary numeric summary, selected-item summary, or bounded interactive\n  field. Before adding a fill, border, radius, or shadow to any layout container,\n  either use `.card` or leave it transparent and unframed; never recreate card\n  chrome on rows, panels, tiles, sections, or wrappers. Keep charts, maps,\n  diagrams, tables, controls, and the whole visualization unframed. Never nest\n  cards; show 2-4 summaries near the top only when useful. Structural groupings\n  and repeated content are not bounded interactive fields. Organize them with\n  layout or visual marks, not container chrome.\n- `.viz-stat`: Use a summary `.card` with one muted label, one\n  `.viz-stat-value`, and at most one short context or delta line.\n- `.viz-grid`: Use for peer metrics or choices instead of a custom grid. It\n  creates as many equal-width columns as fit and stacks when narrow. Never use it\n  for the whole visual or a horizontally scrolling card row. Keep groups to 2-3\n  columns at 736px and controls in a separate row.\n- `.viz-row`: Use as a wrapping horizontal group with centered related values or\n  inline actions that may wrap when narrow.\n- `.progress` + `.progress-bar`:\n  `<div class=\"progress\" role=\"progressbar\" aria-label=\"Progress\" aria-valuenow=\"25\" aria-valuemin=\"0\" aria-valuemax=\"100\"><div class=\"progress-bar\" style=\"width:25%\"></div></div>`\n- `.viz-tile`: Add to a selectable dense-grid `.btn`; it stretches to fill its\n  grid cell, preserves category fill, and uses an accent ring instead of solid\n  selection. Never add another selected, pressed, border, outline, or shadow\n  rule.\n- `.viz-badge`: Use as a compact display-only accent pill for a short status,\n  category, or value; never as a button.\n- `.viz-controls`: Use as a wrapping row for controls affecting the same\n  visualization. Keep button groups compact. Put labeled fields directly inside\n  as `.form-label`; fields form at most two columns and stack when narrow.\n\n### Controls\n\n- `.btn`: Use for a content-sized secondary action. Add `.btn-primary` for one\n  main action per control group or `.btn-ghost` for low emphasis.\n- `.btn-block`: Add to a `.btn` only when the action should intentionally fill\n  the available inline space. Never use it for ordinary row actions.\n- `<a>`: Use for links. Add `.btn` to style a link as a button.\n- `[data-tooltip]`: Use for concise supplementary plain text on static or dynamic\n  triggers; the sandbox handles hover, focus, and touch and creates `.tooltip`\n  elements. Keep essential content visible and triggers labeled. Never use\n  `title`, custom markup, or initialization. Example:\n  `<button type=\"button\" data-tooltip=\"Reset view\">Reset</button>`.\n- `[data-tooltip-placement]`: Optionally prefer `top` (default), `right`,\n  `bottom`, or `left`; collision handling may flip it.\n- `.form-check`: Prefer a wrapping `<label class=\"form-check\">` around the\n  native `.form-check-input` and `.form-check-label` text so the whole row is\n  tappable. An explicit label with matching `for` and input `id` also works.\n- `.form-switch`: Add to `.form-check` around a native checkbox.\n- `.form-control`: Pair a native text, date, file, or color input—or a\n  textarea—with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-control-color`: Add to `.form-control` for a compact native color\n  input.\n- `.form-select`: Pair a native select with `.form-label`.\n- `.form-range`: Pair a native range with a visible label; put its current value\n  and units immediately before it.\n\n### Tables\n\n- `.table`: Use on a semantic table for a quiet, unframed data view. It provides\n  wrapping cells and subtle horizontal dividers without vertical gridlines. Use\n  sentence case for headers.\n- `.table-responsive`: Wrap a table when its columns cannot fit at narrow\n  widths. It contains horizontal overflow without clipping the visualization.\n- `.table-sm`: Add to `.table` when more rows need to fit; it reduces cell\n  padding without shrinking text.\n- `.text-end`, `.text-center`, and `.text-nowrap`: Use inside `.table` for\n  numeric/end alignment, centered values, or values that must stay on one line.\n  Numeric cells use tabular figures when end-aligned.\n\n### Text\n\n- `.text-small`: Use for the smallest host-scaled secondary chart labels and\n  annotations, never below 11px or for essential content.\n- `.text-muted`: Use for secondary units, captions, timestamps, and context,\n  never essential values or labels.\n- `.text-destructive`: Use only for error or validation text the user needs to\n  notice or act on.\n- `<code>`: Use for inline commands, file names, symbols, or short references;\n  put multiline code in `<pre><code>`.\n- `.sr-only`: Use for visually hidden accessible text.\n\n## Charts\n\n- Prefer inline SVG for simple charts and version-pinned approved-CDN\n  libraries when native interaction, scales, legends, or layout materially\n  improve the result.\n- Resolve theme colors before passing them to canvas or chart APIs that cannot\n  parse CSS variables or `light-dark(...)`; redraw when the theme changes.\n- Use a tooltip unless it would distract from a simple, directly labeled chart.\n  Keep chart-library tooltips and grouped legend interactions native; never\n  replace them with a custom one-point tooltip. For SVG, attach `data-tooltip`\n  directly to the real pointer-accessible mark and include its label, value,\n  and units; the sandbox handles themed positioning, keyboard focus, and touch.\n- Animate transitions between chart states so lines and marks move to their new\n  values, resampling paths when point counts differ. Do not animate initial\n  appearance or use fade-only effects; never loop motion, and honor\n  `prefers-reduced-motion`.\n- Scope SVG styles to the chart class. Never target every `svg` in a container\n  that also contains Lucide icons.\n- Include labeled axes, units, and directly labeled important values. Give every\n  chart, SVG, canvas, and widget a concise screen-reader summary using a role and\n  accessible name or description, SVG `<title>`/`<desc>`, fallback text, or an\n  `.sr-only` heading or description.\n- Reserve space for the longest formatted label at every supported width. Axis\n  ticks are secondary and may use `.text-small` when space is tight. Never\n  overlap or clip text against marks, axes, legends, labels, or edges; move or\n  reduce labels rather than squeeze them.\n- Add a legend only when multiple series cannot be labeled directly.\n- Pair color with shape or text so meaning never depends on color alone.\n\n## Icons and mockups\n\n- Use the sandbox-provided global `lucide`. Add an icon name with `data-lucide`:\n\n  ```html\n  <i data-lucide=\"search\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>\n  ```\n\n- Lucide replaces the placeholder in place with an inline SVG. Icons are 16px\n  and inherit `currentColor`.\n- Mark decorative icons `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. Put action icons inside labeled\n  controls; use a visible label or `aria-label` for icon-only actions.\n- Let the sandbox initialize static icons after the fragment without blocking\n  first render. After adding icons dynamically, use\n  `lucide.createIcons({ attrs: { width: 16, height: 16 } })`.\n- Never load Lucide or another icon library from the network.\n- Use visibly labeled buttons and inputs for small interactions. Keep all\n  presentation-only interaction local to the fragment and make the first render\n  useful before input changes.\n- Use semantic controls, realistic spacing, and restrained chrome for mockups.\n  Never fake product screenshots when inspectable UI is needed.\n   691\tscope: Use for DATA-1/AUTH-1-style canonical-gateway issue delivery from clean `origin/dev` worktrees through bounded review, exact-head merge, and cleanup.\n   692\tapplies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev; reuse_rule=worktree, approval, and exact-head procedures are reusable, but code, PR, CI, and deployment evidence are snapshot-specific.\n   693\t\n   694\t## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees, success\n   695\t\n   696\t### rollout_summary_files\n   697\t\n   698\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, DATA-1 and AUTH-1 implemented, merged, and cleaned up)\n   699\t\n   700\t### keywords\n   701\t\n   702\t- DATA-1, AUTH-1, canonical-gateway, origin/dev, 72aabce, placement, CAS, HMAC assertions, replay protection, single-writer-strong, PR #121, PR #120, 1bf6f6bb\n   703\t\n   704\t## Task 2: Refetch and classify AUTH-1 PR review feedback once, success\n   705\t\n   706\t### rollout_summary_files\n   707\t\n   708\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, all 14 unresolved threads were stale or already fixed)\n   709\t\n   710\t### keywords\n   711\t\n   712\t- splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1, GH_CONFIG_DIR, reviewThreads, isOutdated, exact PR head, --match-head-commit, bf3a19d1, Node 22\n   713\t\n   714\t## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 then DATA-1 into dev, success\n   715\t\n   716\t### rollout_summary_files\n   717\t\n   718\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, exact-head merges completed and final origin/dev verified)\n   719\t\n   720\t### keywords\n   721\t\n   722\t- gh pr merge, --match-head-commit, origin/dev, git ort, 454c40f8, 3158fc83, e3b9834c, npm --workspace datafn/server test, git diff --check\n   723\t\n   724\t## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees, success\n   725\t\n   726\t### rollout_summary_files\n   727\t\n   728\t- rollout_summaries/REDACTED.md (cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev, rollout_path=/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl, updated_at=2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00, thread_id=01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af, dedicated worktrees removed without touching others)\n   729\t\n   730\t### keywords\n   731\t\n   732\t- git worktree remove, DATA-1, AUTH-1, process timeout, git worktree list, cleanup\n   733\t\n   734\t## User preferences\n   735\t\n   736\t- when the user requests new worktrees “off `origin/dev`” -> preserve the dirty checkout, create an isolated worktree from the authoritative exact ref, and keep requested scopes separate. [Task 1]\n   737\t- when the user requests end-to-end work in new worktrees -> preserve the dirty checkout, use freshly fetched `origin/dev` worktrees, and carry publication/verification through only when authorized. [Task 1]\n   738\t- when the user invokes `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> perform one bounded round: unresolved GraphQL threads first, then reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; validate against the exact worktree; push at most once; compare heads once; stop without post-push polling. [Task 2]\n   739\t\n   740\t## Reusable knowledge\n   741\t\n   742\t- DATA-1 PR #121 and AUTH-1 PR #120 were merged into `dev`; placement ownership remains separate from permission-grant projections/outboxes, while AUTH-1 keeps direct-mode compatibility alongside canonical gateway routing. [Task 1][Task 3]\n   743\t- DynamoDB placement uses `single-writer-strong` consistency and consistent reads; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic. Routing assertions bind identity plus relevant method/path/body data; stale routing may retry only once before execution. [Task 1]\n   744\t- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`. Review every unresolved thread against `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head; timestamp-only comment queries are insufficient. [Task 2]\n   745\t- Exact merge pattern: merge the first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch resulting `origin/dev`, merge it into the dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, reread PR head, merge with an exact-head guard, then verify ancestry in `origin/dev`. DATA-1 validation: 92 files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 3]\n   746\t\n   747\t## Failures and how to do differently\n   748\t\nthread_id: 01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af\nupdated_at: 2026-08-25T16:25:10+00:00\nrollout_path: /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/08/23/rollout-2026-08-23T23-21-55-01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af.jsonl\ncwd: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\ngit_branch: SFNS-2\n\n# DATA-1 and AUTH-1 were implemented, reviewed, merged, and their dedicated worktrees removed\n\nRollout context: The user requested end-to-end work on Linear issues DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in separate worktrees based on freshly fetched `origin/dev`, followed by PR review remediation, merging, and cleanup. The original dirty checkout `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev` was preserved. Hosted Skillplane workflow `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1` was retrieved and followed.\n\n## Task 1: Implement DATA-1 and AUTH-1 in isolated worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user asked to “work DATA-1 and AUTH-1 issues in a new worktrees off of origin/dev end to end” -> preserve the existing checkout, use separate clean worktrees from the authoritative fetched ref, and keep issue scopes isolated.\n- The workflow required local implementation and verification before publication approval, then exact issue-ID branches for publication.\n\nKey steps:\n- Read both complete Linear issues, all comments, linked architecture/rationale documents, relations, and project metadata.\n- Fetched `origin/dev` at `72aabce915426325450663feffd7a53089ef4489` and created detached worktrees under `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1` and `AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 implemented tenant placement separate from permission indexing, atomic placement/CAS epoch moves, gateway dispatch, signed assertions, pre-execution fencing, bounded retry, sync/WebSocket behavior, migration fencing, observability, provider-neutral adapters, tests, and documentation.\n- AUTH-1 implemented canonical gateway routing with direct-mode compatibility, identity placement/CAS, signed HMAC assertions and replay protection, pre-side-effect routing, TypeScript/Python adapter support, OAuth/session semantics, deletion fencing, migration behavior, Swift/client updates, tests, and documentation.\n- DATA-1 was published as PR #121 and AUTH-1 as PR #120. DATA-1 later received one narrow remediation fixing placement validation order before custom-route middleware; commit `1bf6f6bb` was pushed and verified against the PR head.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- The original checkout was dirty with unrelated `.conduct` artifacts; do not clean or modify it during similar work.\n- AUTH-1’s initial remote CI had unrelated repository failures, including a FastAPI `StopIteration` issue in prior history; distinguish local feature validation from repository-wide CI and live multi-region proof.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Placement ownership must remain a separate source of truth from permission-grant projections/outboxes.\n- Routing assertions should bind request identity and relevant method/path/body data, and stale routing may retry only once before execution.\n- DynamoDB placement used `single-writer-strong` consistency; Global Table replicas are not globally atomic.\n\nReferences:\n- Hosted workflow: `21n/work-linear-issue@1.0.1`, digest prefix `716e55…abaf`.\n- Worktrees: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`, `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1`.\n- DATA-1 PR #121: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/121`.\n- AUTH-1 PR #120: `https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/120`.\n\n## Task 2: Bounded AUTH-1 PR review remediation\n\nOutcome: success\n\nPreference signals:\n- The user invoked `splane pr-refetch-fix AUTH-1` -> use one bounded review round: fetch unresolved GraphQL threads plus reviews, issue comments, inline comments, and checks; classify against the exact PR worktree; push at most once; verify heads once; do not refetch or poll afterward.\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 worktree was clean and local, remote, and PR heads matched `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- Frozen review snapshot contained 56 threads, 14 unresolved, 10 with current comments, 4 outdated-only threads, 49 reviews, 4 issue comments, 54 inline comments, and 28 checks.\n- Verified all unresolved findings against current code. Middleware execution, `/runtime` compatibility/global classification, cell-region registration precedence, deletion fencing, error propagation, and related issues were already fixed or stale. No edit, commit, or push was needed.\n- Recorded unrelated failed CI causes: DevFn required Node 22 but CI used Node 20; BillFn and SQLAlchemy had pre-existing typing issues; python-core had Ruff import formatting failures.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Do not treat every unresolved GitHub thread as actionable; inspect `isOutdated`, current code, and the exact PR head.\n- Do not use timestamp-only comment queries; unresolved historical threads can remain actionable, while many current-looking threads are stale.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- For private GitHub operations use `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other`.\n- Use exact-head guards such as `--match-head-commit`; after an authorized push, compare local, remote, and live PR SHAs once and stop.\n\nReferences:\n- Skill: `/Users/serro/.codex/skills/pr-refetch-fix/SKILL.md`.\n- AUTH-1 final reviewed head: `bf3a19d192741f0ef19c676cb37e7713a1794aaa`.\n- AUTH-1 PR review result: 0 fixes; all 14 unresolved threads stale/already fixed.\n\n## Task 3: Merge AUTH-1 and DATA-1 into dev\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed both PRs were clean and mergeable; merge commits were enabled.\n- Merged AUTH-1 first with an exact-head guard. PR #120 merged at `REDACTED`.\n- Fetched the resulting `origin/dev` and merged it into DATA-1. Git’s `ort` merge completed without conflicts; no manual conflict resolution was required.\n- Ran the combined DATA-1 server suite: 92 test files, 1,007 passed, 1 skipped; DataFn typecheck, build, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Pushed DATA-1 merge commit `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`, verified local/remote/PR heads, then merged PR #121 at `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Verified both PR heads are ancestors of final `origin/dev` `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- Refresh a dependent PR against the actual post-merge `origin/dev` before merging it. This avoided guessing about overlap and confirmed whether conflicts existed.\n\nReusable knowledge:\n- Exact merge pattern: inspect PR state/heads, merge first PR with `--match-head-commit`, fetch `origin/dev`, merge into dependent worktree, run focused validation, push once, re-read PR head, merge with exact-head guard, and verify ancestry in `origin/dev`.\n\nReferences:\n- AUTH-1 merge commit: `REDACTED`.\n- DATA-1 branch merge commit before PR merge: `3158fc83f2c072bc0a6225cb9e3350abaef92e0e`.\n- Final `origin/dev`: `e3b9834c599fe513fb38dd8154299a76d1162309`.\n- Validation: `npm --workspace datafn/server test`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run typecheck`, `npm --workspace datafn/server run build`, `git diff --check`.\n\n## Task 4: Remove associated local worktrees\n\nOutcome: success\n\nKey steps:\n- Confirmed AUTH-1 and DATA-1 worktrees were clean and registered.\n- Ran Git worktree removal for both paths. The first command exceeded its execution window, but the process was checked and allowed to finish rather than racing a second cleanup.\n- Verified both directories were absent, Git no longer listed them, and local branches remained available: AUTH-1 at `bf3a19d1…`, DATA-1 at `3158fc83…`.\n- Other worktrees were untouched.\n\nFailures and how to do differently:\n- `git worktree remove` may take longer than the command timeout on large repositories. Check for an active process before retrying.\n\nReferences:\n- Removed: `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/AUTH-1` and `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/DATA-1`.\n- Remaining branches: `AUTH-1`, `DATA-1`.\n\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","create_time":1787984670.05136}}}
