{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:47.446Z","ordinal":12123,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0eef853664611d0c016a927b2e573887d0ac01a20149950a5a","status":"completed","call_id":"call_PxEWMqV79eQw1wJnQWHURAru","name":"exec","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd:\"set -e\\nsed -n '1,260p' datafn/docs/content/docs/documentation/multi-region-gateway.mdx\\nsed -n '1,300p' authfn/docs/content/docs/recipes/canonical-gateway-multi-region.md\",\n  workdir:\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\",\n  yield_time_ms:10000,\n  max_output_tokens:30000\n});\ntext(r.output);\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","create_time":1787984683.817216}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:47.523Z","ordinal":12124,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af","turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6841951d-1249-4bb9-b204-01bd012b532e","process_id":"25427","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","set -e\nsed -n '1,260p' datafn/docs/content/docs/documentation/multi-region-gateway.mdx\nsed -n '1,300p' authfn/docs/content/docs/recipes/canonical-gateway-multi-region.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"set -e\nsed -n '1,260p' datafn/docs/content/docs/documentation/multi-region-gateway.mdx\nsed -n '1,300p' authfn/docs/content/docs/recipes/canonical-gateway-multi-region.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\ntitle: Multi-region gateway routing\ndescription: Tenant placement, regional cell fencing, canonical ingress, safe retries, and namespace migration.\n---\n\n# Multi-region gateway routing\n\nDataFn supports an opt-in canonical-gateway model for applications that keep each tenant namespace in one writable regional cell. Clients keep one `remote` and `wsUrl`; regional destinations remain internal deployment handles.\n\nThis capability does not provide active-active writes or automatic failover to an empty region. Existing single-region configurations and permission-directory grant indexing are unchanged unless a placement runtime is configured.\n\n## Separate ownership from permission projections\n\n`DatafnPlacementDirectoryAdapter` is the authority for namespace ownership. Its records contain a namespace, owning `regionId`, monotonically increasing `epoch`, lifecycle state, update time, and optional cache/destination metadata. `get`, `putIfAbsent`, and `compareAndSet` must all be linearizable for a namespace; for DynamoDB-backed atomic KV stores, configure strong reads (`consistentRead: true`).\n\nThe indexed permission directory is a separate projection. Permission-directory lag can affect sharing discovery, but it never changes a namespace's database owner. Migration must drain its outbox and rebuild the target projection explicitly.\n\nThe built-in `createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory` composes the focused placement contract with any `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` that implements `compareAndSet`. Its required `consistencyModel: \"linearizable\"` acknowledgement makes the deployment contract explicit: DynamoDB adapters must enable `consistentRead: true`, and other providers must route reads and CAS writes through one linearizable authority. Memory, Redis, and Cloudflare Durable Object stores can use the same contract without placing provider APIs in DataFn core.\n\n`createMemoryDatafnPlacementDirectory` is for tests and one-process deployments only. Migrations that must recover after a process crash require a durable placement directory so recovery ownership, fencing tokens, and lease state survive the restart.\n\n## Regional cell\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory,\n  createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions,\n  createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore,\n  createDatafnServer,\n  datafnMultiRegionPlugin,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst assertions = createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions({\n  activeKeyId: \"2026-08\",\n  keys: { \"2026-08\": env.DATAFN_ROUTING_KEY },\n});\nconst replayStore = createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore(); // tests and one-process deployments only\n\nconst placement = createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory(\n  stores.atomicKv,\n  { consistencyModel: \"linearizable\" },\n);\n\nconst server = await createDatafnServer({\n  schema,\n  database: regionalDatabase,\n  context: authenticateRequest,\n  namespaceProvider: {\n    getNamespace: (context) => context.authorizedNamespace,\n  },\n  plugins: [\n    datafnMultiRegionPlugin({\n      regionId: \"eu-west\",\n      directory: permissionDirectory,\n      placement: {\n        directory: placement,\n        requireRoutingAssertion: true,\n        assertionVerifier: assertions,\n        replayStore,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nThe server derives the trusted namespace from authenticated context, verifies the gateway assertion, and checks region + epoch before plugin authorization, application authorization, or a database/sync operation. The built-in payload containment guard may reject internal-resource references before this placement check; it does not perform route or application authorization. Built-in HTTP, REST, sync, in-process search, public-link routes, and routed WebSocket handshakes use this fence. Health status remains cell-local and does not require a tenant placement.\n\nCustom plugin routes must declare `meta.datafnPlacement.resolveNamespace`. The resolver either returns the authenticated namespace or an early `Response`. An undeclared custom route is rejected at startup in placement mode. Routes that are proven global and do not touch regional data may declare `datafnPlacement: \"none\"`.\n\n## Canonical gateway\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createDatafnGatewayRouter,\n  withDatafnRoutingAssertion,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst gateway = createDatafnGatewayRouter({\n  directory: placement,\n  deriveNamespace: async (request) =>\n    (await authenticateRequest(request)).authorizedNamespace,\n  cellRegistry: {\n    resolve: ({ regionId, destinationRef }) =>\n      internalCells.resolve(regionId, destinationRef),\n  },\n  assertionSigner: assertions,\n  dispatcher: {\n    // HTTP-origin example. Adapt this call when resolve() returns an RPC stub,\n    // service binding, or function reference instead of a fetch-capable target.\n    dispatch: ({ target, request, assertion }) =>\n      target.fetch(withDatafnRoutingAssertion(request, assertion)),\n  },\n  maxCacheEntries: 10_000,\n});\n```\n\nThe gateway strips caller-supplied internal headers, preserves the method, complete path and query string, body, cookies, authorization, correlation/idempotency headers, and request ID, then signs namespace, region, epoch, method/request target, body presence and digest, audience, timestamps, and a nonce. It buffers and hashes at most 5 MiB before invoking even a body-reading namespace resolver, so resolver clones cannot accumulate an unbounded stream; set `maxBodyBytes` on the gateway and placement runtime when a deployment uses another limit. The placement cache also has an insertion-ordered bound (`maxCacheEntries`, default 10,000) in addition to its TTL. Each cell atomically consumes the signed nonce through a shared replay store before execution. Assertion-enabled placement configuration fails at startup unless it supplies both a verifier and replay store. The in-memory replay store is for tests and one-process deployments only; production cells sharing an audience need a bounded durable store with atomic first claim. The destination resolver may return an HTTP origin, service binding, RPC stub, or function reference, and the dispatcher must adapt the request for that target type; destination details are never serialized into a public response.\n\nOn an authenticated, guaranteed pre-execution `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH`, the gateway invalidates its cache, re-resolves placement, and retries exactly once. It does not replay ambiguous timeouts or responses produced after execution began. Existing mutation IDs and idempotency keys remain authoritative for application-level retries.\n\n## Errors and availability\n\n| Code | Meaning | Default behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_NOT_FOUND` | No active placement or a tombstone | Fail closed; do not choose a default region |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_UNAVAILABLE` | Directory read failed | Retryable 503; a miss is not inferred |\n| `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH` | Wrong cell or stale epoch before execution | Internal metadata may trigger one gateway retry |\n| `DATAFN_NAMESPACE_MOVING` | The move fence is active | Retryable 409; no cell accepts new work |\n| `DATAFN_CELL_UNAVAILABLE` | Destination resolution or dispatch failed | Pre-send resolution failures are retryable; ambiguous dispatch failures report `executionStarted: true` and are not retry-safe |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_ASSERTION_INVALID` | Spoofed, expired, or mismatched assertion | Non-retryable 401 |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_RETRY_EXHAUSTED` | The bounded re-resolution was insufficient | Retryable 409; operator review may be required |\n| `DATAFN_PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` | The gateway body limit was exceeded | Non-retryable 413 before dispatch |\n\nOnly a verified internal assertion receives region/epoch hints. Public direct responses do not reveal internal topology.\n\n## WebSocket and sync epochs\n\nUse `server.websocketHandler.addRoutedClient(client, authContext, handshakeRequest)` in placement mode. Routed clients must expose a transport-level `close` operation so fencing can deliver code `4510` before removing the session. The handshake pins namespace, region, and epoch. The legacy synchronous `addClient` rejects connections while placement mode is enabled so an adapter cannot accidentally bypass the fence.\n\nWhen a move starts, call `fenceNamespace(namespace)` (or provide the new minimum epoch) on the source cell. Each fence advances a namespace generation, so a handshake that was still awaiting placement validation cannot register after the fence completes. Stale sessions close with code `4510` and reconnect through the canonical `wsUrl`. Mutation IDs, pull cursors, and reconcile checkpoints remain valid only if the migration copy/validation hooks preserve them; otherwise the application must fail explicitly and require a fresh clone.\n\n## Migration protocol\n\n`migrateDatafnNamespace` implements the minimal fenced order:\n\n1. CAS the active placement to `moving` at a newer epoch.\n2. Quiesce source writes and close/fence old sessions.\n3. Drain permission-directory outbox work.\n4. Copy and validate tenant data, cursors, mutation IDs, and checkpoints.\n5. Rebuild the permission projection and warm the target.\n6. CAS the moving record to a target `active` placement with explicit `resume-target` recovery state at another epoch.\n7. Resume target writes, then clear the recovery state through one final epoch CAS.\n\nFailure before activation CASes to an active source placement with explicit `resume-source` recovery state. Gateway and cell traffic remain fenced until the required `rollbackSource` hook succeeds and a final epoch CAS clears that state; `migrateDatafnNamespace` rejects configurations without that hook before it fences writes. A failed or interrupted rollback can therefore be claimed and retried without publishing a quiesced source. The source rollback hook runs only if the first rollback CAS succeeds. A durable placement directory is required to persist the recovery owner, monotonic `recoveryFence`, and expiry lease across crashes; do not use the in-memory directory for migrations that must survive process restarts. After a crash, one retry claims the expired lease through CAS before continuing. Every external hook boundary must atomically compare and persist `recoveryFence` in the affected system, rejecting lower tokens before applying an effect; timer renewal alone cannot fence an already-running hook after takeover. `recoveryLeaseExpiresAt` is a live getter that reflects successful renewals, but it is scheduling guidance rather than a substitute for the fencing token. Hooks must remain idempotent. `resume-source` and `resume-target` placements remain fenced from gateway and cell traffic until their resume hook succeeds and the final CAS clears recovery state. Set `recoveryLeaseMs` longer than the maximum expected hook interval. A same-region destination-only change is rejected instead of silently ignoring it. Rollback after activation is another explicit migration; never edit a cache, region map, or DNS record out of band.\n\n## Observability and operations\n\nProvide `onEvent` to collect placement lookup/cache hit, forward, mismatch, retry, moving, directory unavailable, destination unavailable, epoch conflict, and assertion failure events. Hash namespace identifiers before sending them to shared telemetry. Monitor directory availability and latency, cross-region dispatch latency, retry exhaustion, assertion failures, permission-outbox backlog, and migration stalls.\n\nRun shadow placement checks before enforcement, enable gateway routing for test tenants, and expand gradually. Keep the original single-region configuration as rollback only until the first placement move; after that, rollback must use the epoch protocol.\n---\ntitle: Canonical-gateway multi-region\ndescription: One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n---\n\n# Canonical-gateway multi-region\n\nUse this mode when every browser, OAuth provider, native app, and API client must see one authority such as `https://account.example.com`, while identity state remains pinned to a regional cell.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe gateway, placement directory, cell registry, dispatcher, and signing-key resolver are server-side infrastructure. Never accept an identity key, region, epoch, cell URL, or internal routing header as a trusted client hint.\n\nThe gateway:\n\n1. Classifies the route and derives a stable identity key from trusted request material. Email/password and OTP flows may use a normalized, keyed email digest; session, refresh, revoke, API-key, OAuth-callback, handoff, deletion, and email-change flows should use a verified opaque routing handle.\n2. Reads or atomically claims `{ identityKey, regionId, epoch, state }` in the placement directory.\n3. Resolves the region through a private cell registry. The registry owns the destination handle; placement never contains a URL or service binding.\n4. Removes every incoming `x-authfn-routing-*` header and creates a short-lived HMAC assertion bound to identity, region, epoch, request id, method, path and query, audience, nonce, and request-body digest.\n5. Dispatches internally. A cell verifies the assertion, atomically claims its nonce, and rereads placement before rate limiting, authentication hooks, database reads/writes, OTP delivery, token issuance, or any other auth side effect.\n\nThe cell returns a signed mismatch only when it can prove `executionStarted: false`. The gateway refreshes placement and retries exactly once. Other failures, an unsigned mismatch, a second mismatch, or any response after execution began are returned without retry.\n\n## TypeScript setup\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  authFnMultiRegionEnvironment,\n  authFnMultiRegionPlugin,\n  createAuthFnCanonicalGateway,\n  createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore,\n} from '@authfn/multi-region';\nimport { authFnPlugins, authfn } from 'authfn';\nimport { createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory } from '@authfn/lookup-dynamodb';\nimport type { Adapter } from '@superfunctions/db';\n\nconst placementWriterRegion = process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_WRITER_REGION!;\nconst placementDirectory = createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory({\n  tableName: process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_TABLE!,\n  consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong',\n  writerRegion: placementWriterRegion,\n  region: placementWriterRegion,\n});\n\nconst keyring = {\n  active: {\n    keyId: process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!,\n    secret: await secrets.resolve(process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!),\n  },\n  previous: await secrets.resolveVerificationKeys(),\n};\n\n// Configure this independently in each regional cell.\nconst environment = authFnMultiRegionEnvironment({\n  regions,\n  routing: {\n    mode: 'gateway',\n    publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n    canonicalCookie: { prefix: 'authfn', domain: '.example.com', sameSite: 'lax' },\n    canonicalOAuth: oauthConfig,\n    placementDirectory,\n    identityKeyForIdentifier: identityKeys.fromEmail,\n    identityKeyForUserId: identityKeys.fromUserId,\n    cell: {\n      regionId: process.env.REGION_ID!,\n      audience: `authfn-cell:${process.env.REGION_ID}`,\n      keyring,\n      replayStore: createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore(), // use an atomic shared store per cell in production\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nexport function createRegionalCell(database: Adapter) {\n  return authfn({\n    plugins: authFnPlugins(authFnMultiRegionPlugin()),\n  }).createServer({ database, environment });\n}\n\nconst gateway = createAuthFnCanonicalGateway({\n  publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n  basePath: '/auth',\n  placementDirectory,\n  keyring,\n  resolveIdentity: resolveTrustedIdentity, // set allowInitialPlacement only for approved first-use routes\n  selectInitialRegion: placementPolicy.select,\n  resolveCell: cellRegistry.resolve, // { regionId, audience, target: opaqueBinding }\n  dispatch: (target, request) => internalTransport.fetch(target, request),\n  handleGlobal: globalAuthMetadata.handle,\n});\n```\n\nThe internal dispatcher must preserve cookies, CSRF headers, and the signed `x-request-id`. Normalize `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For`, and equivalent client-IP headers at the canonical ingress according to one documented trusted-proxy policy; do not accept an arbitrary client-provided forwarding chain.\n\nAn AuthFn runtime configured for gateway mode without `routing.cell` is gateway-only: discovery and the non-enumerating lookup route may execute there, while every identity-scoped route fails closed. Regional runtimes add `routing.cell` and are reachable only through a valid signed assertion.\n\n`createCloudflareIdentityPlacementDirectory` provides the same placement contract over a key-named Durable Object. The DynamoDB convenience adapter deliberately requires `consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong'` and forces consistent reads: every cell and gateway must address the same writer region. Local DynamoDB Global Table replicas are eventually consistent with one another and do not, by themselves, provide globally atomic first claims or epoch moves. Global Tables may replicate the directory for recovery or shadow reads, but routing decisions must not use a local replica until a stronger coordinator establishes ownership. `createStoreBackedAuthFnPlacementDirectory` composes placement over another `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` only when that adapter implements atomic `compareAndSet` across every writer.\n\nPython exposes the equivalent `CanonicalGateway`, `CanonicalRoutingConfig`, `InMemoryIdentityPlacementDirectory`, `InMemoryRoutingReplayStore`, `RoutingKeyring`, `create_cell_routing_middleware`, and `move_identity_placement` contracts from `authfn.plugins.gateway_routing`.\n\n## Route ownership matrix\n\n| Route family | Gateway action | Identity source | Cell behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Discovery and `/regions/lookup` | Terminate globally with the canonical authority and no existence/region signal. | None | Not consulted. |\n| Password and OTP start/verify/reset | Route. Atomically claim on first use if policy allows. | Normalized keyed identifier derived from the body. | Validate before lookup, challenge creation, delivery, verification, or session issue. |\n| Session read/list/revoke/sign-out | Route. | Verified opaque session routing handle. | Validate before session reads, last-seen updates, or revocation. |\n| API-key create/list/revoke/authenticate | Route. | Verified session handle or API-key routing prefix/MAC. | Validate before key lookup or mutation. |\n| Social OAuth start, GET/POST callback, disconnect, and native Apple start/complete | Route. | Verified session, OAuth state, or native handshake routing handle. | Keep issuer and callback canonical; validate before state consumption or identity mutation. |\n| Native/web handoff | Route. | Signed handoff routing handle. | Validate before code creation or exchange. |\n| Account deletion and email change | Route. | Verified session handle for self-service; `identityKeyForUserId` for an administrator's target, independent of whether the admin authorizer supplies an `actorId`. | Validate routing before mutation. Account deletion CAS-fences `active` to durable `deleting` before the cascade, restores `active` if the cascade fails, and publishes `tombstoned` only after success. A second deletion attempt is rejected while that fence exists, preventing concurrent cascades from restoring or finalizing each other's state. After inspecting the cascade outcome, an operator may repair a stranded fence with `restoreAuthFnIdentityDeletion` or `finalizeAuthFnIdentityDeletion`; email-change flows must update placement with an application-level transaction/outbox protocol. |\n\nDo not fall back to a default cell for an established identity whose placement is absent. Initial placement is only for explicitly classified first-use flows. Public lookup responses must be identical for present and absent identities.\n\n## Migration and backfill\n\nBackfill existing users by deriving the same stable identity key used at the gateway and calling `putIfAbsent` with the authoritative current region at epoch 1. Treat a conflicting existing record as an incident; never overwrite it with an unconditional write.\n\n`moveAuthFnIdentityPlacement` performs the minimum fenced sequence:\n\n1. CAS `active(source, epoch N)` to `moving(source → target, N+1)`.\n2. Quiesce source writes and drain in-flight auth/delivery work.\n3. Copy identity state and dependent credentials/tokens, validate it, and warm the target.\n4. Resume target capacity and complete readiness checks while public identity traffic remains fenced by `moving`, then CAS to `active(target, N+2)`. Publishing `active` before the target is fully ready would expose an unroutable owner.\n5. Before activation, failures leave placement fenced as `moving` unless a source recovery callback is supplied. With source recovery, resume the source first and only then CAS to `active(source, N+2)`; a failed source resume must never publish an active source. After target activation, ownership remains at the target.\n\nInvalidate gateway placement caches after every claim, move, tombstone, and operator repair. Old assertions are rejected by epoch even when their TTL has not elapsed.\n\n## Availability and fallback\n\n- Placement directory unavailable: fail closed with `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_DIRECTORY_UNAVAILABLE`; do not guess a region.\n- Placement `moving`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; do not start auth side effects.\n- Placement `deleting`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; the durable deletion fence must be finalized or restored before identity traffic resumes.\n- Missing/tombstoned established placement: fail closed. Do not enumerate the identity through the public lookup route.\n- Cell missing or internal dispatch failure: `AUTHFN_ROUTING_CELL_UNAVAILABLE`.\n- Gateway outage: restore the canonical gateway. Do not expose regional authorities as an automatic fallback because that changes issuer, callback, cookie, and topology contracts.\n- Direct mode remains a separately configured compatibility mode for existing regional clients.\n\n## Key rotation and secret handling\n\nKeep routing keys in a KMS/HSM-backed secret resolver. Never place raw keys, assertions, identity keys, OTPs, cookies, authorization codes, or token bodies in logs. Rotation is add → sign → retire:\n\n1. Add the new key to every cell's verification keyring.\n2. Switch the gateway's `active.keyId` and signer.\n3. Wait longer than assertion TTL plus clock skew and deployment propagation.\n4. Remove the retired verification key.\n\nEvery cell uses an atomic replay store. The in-memory implementation is for tests or a single process; multi-instance cells need shared nonce claims with TTL.\n\n## SLOs, dashboards, and drills\n\nTrack placement lookup latency/availability, claim/CAS conflicts, cache hit rate, dispatch latency by cell, signed mismatch rate, retry success/exhaustion, assertion rejection reason, replay rejection, moving duration, and cell-unavailable errors. Suggested starting objectives are 99.99% directory availability, p99 placement lookup under 50 ms, and fewer than 0.1% routed requests requiring the stale-placement retry; tune these to your infrastructure.\n\nRun these drills before rollout and quarterly afterward:\n\n- deny directory reads and confirm no regional handler or delivery provider runs;\n- remove a cell binding and confirm the error contains no internal target;\n- replay and body-modify a captured assertion and confirm rejection;\n- move an identity during OTP, session refresh, OAuth callback, and handoff flows;\n- fail each migration callback before activation and verify placement stays fenced unless source recovery succeeds;\n- fail target resume and source resume independently, and verify neither region is published active before it is ready;\n- rotate the signing key with mixed-version gateway/cell deployments;\n- verify discovery issuer, OAuth redirect URI, cookie names/domain, browser origins, and native return paths remain canonical before and after a move.\n\n## Rollout\n\nStart with shadow placement reads and mismatch metrics, backfill deterministically, enable signed validation in cells, then send a small canonical-gateway cohort. Increase traffic only after retry exhaustion, assertion rejection, public topology leakage, and issuer/cookie/callback invariants are clean. Roll back gateway traffic routing without deleting placement or disabling cell validation; a later retry can resume from the same epochs.\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: Multi-region gateway routing\ndescription: Tenant placement, regional cell fencing, canonical ingress, safe retries, and namespace migration.\n---\n\n# Multi-region gateway routing\n\nDataFn supports an opt-in canonical-gateway model for applications that keep each tenant namespace in one writable regional cell. Clients keep one `remote` and `wsUrl`; regional destinations remain internal deployment handles.\n\nThis capability does not provide active-active writes or automatic failover to an empty region. Existing single-region configurations and permission-directory grant indexing are unchanged unless a placement runtime is configured.\n\n## Separate ownership from permission projections\n\n`DatafnPlacementDirectoryAdapter` is the authority for namespace ownership. Its records contain a namespace, owning `regionId`, monotonically increasing `epoch`, lifecycle state, update time, and optional cache/destination metadata. `get`, `putIfAbsent`, and `compareAndSet` must all be linearizable for a namespace; for DynamoDB-backed atomic KV stores, configure strong reads (`consistentRead: true`).\n\nThe indexed permission directory is a separate projection. Permission-directory lag can affect sharing discovery, but it never changes a namespace's database owner. Migration must drain its outbox and rebuild the target projection explicitly.\n\nThe built-in `createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory` composes the focused placement contract with any `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` that implements `compareAndSet`. Its required `consistencyModel: \"linearizable\"` acknowledgement makes the deployment contract explicit: DynamoDB adapters must enable `consistentRead: true`, and other providers must route reads and CAS writes through one linearizable authority. Memory, Redis, and Cloudflare Durable Object stores can use the same contract without placing provider APIs in DataFn core.\n\n`createMemoryDatafnPlacementDirectory` is for tests and one-process deployments only. Migrations that must recover after a process crash require a durable placement directory so recovery ownership, fencing tokens, and lease state survive the restart.\n\n## Regional cell\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory,\n  createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions,\n  createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore,\n  createDatafnServer,\n  datafnMultiRegionPlugin,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst assertions = createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions({\n  activeKeyId: \"2026-08\",\n  keys: { \"2026-08\": env.DATAFN_ROUTING_KEY },\n});\nconst replayStore = createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore(); // tests and one-process deployments only\n\nconst placement = createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory(\n  stores.atomicKv,\n  { consistencyModel: \"linearizable\" },\n);\n\nconst server = await createDatafnServer({\n  schema,\n  database: regionalDatabase,\n  context: authenticateRequest,\n  namespaceProvider: {\n    getNamespace: (context) => context.authorizedNamespace,\n  },\n  plugins: [\n    datafnMultiRegionPlugin({\n      regionId: \"eu-west\",\n      directory: permissionDirectory,\n      placement: {\n        directory: placement,\n        requireRoutingAssertion: true,\n        assertionVerifier: assertions,\n        replayStore,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nThe server derives the trusted namespace from authenticated context, verifies the gateway assertion, and checks region + epoch before plugin authorization, application authorization, or a database/sync operation. The built-in payload containment guard may reject internal-resource references before this placement check; it does not perform route or application authorization. Built-in HTTP, REST, sync, in-process search, public-link routes, and routed WebSocket handshakes use this fence. Health status remains cell-local and does not require a tenant placement.\n\nCustom plugin routes must declare `meta.datafnPlacement.resolveNamespace`. The resolver either returns the authenticated namespace or an early `Response`. An undeclared custom route is rejected at startup in placement mode. Routes that are proven global and do not touch regional data may declare `datafnPlacement: \"none\"`.\n\n## Canonical gateway\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createDatafnGatewayRouter,\n  withDatafnRoutingAssertion,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst gateway = createDatafnGatewayRouter({\n  directory: placement,\n  deriveNamespace: async (request) =>\n    (await authenticateRequest(request)).authorizedNamespace,\n  cellRegistry: {\n    resolve: ({ regionId, destinationRef }) =>\n      internalCells.resolve(regionId, destinationRef),\n  },\n  assertionSigner: assertions,\n  dispatcher: {\n    // HTTP-origin example. Adapt this call when resolve() returns an RPC stub,\n    // service binding, or function reference instead of a fetch-capable target.\n    dispatch: ({ target, request, assertion }) =>\n      target.fetch(withDatafnRoutingAssertion(request, assertion)),\n  },\n  maxCacheEntries: 10_000,\n});\n```\n\nThe gateway strips caller-supplied internal headers, preserves the method, complete path and query string, body, cookies, authorization, correlation/idempotency headers, and request ID, then signs namespace, region, epoch, method/request target, body presence and digest, audience, timestamps, and a nonce. It buffers and hashes at most 5 MiB before invoking even a body-reading namespace resolver, so resolver clones cannot accumulate an unbounded stream; set `maxBodyBytes` on the gateway and placement runtime when a deployment uses another limit. The placement cache also has an insertion-ordered bound (`maxCacheEntries`, default 10,000) in addition to its TTL. Each cell atomically consumes the signed nonce through a shared replay store before execution. Assertion-enabled placement configuration fails at startup unless it supplies both a verifier and replay store. The in-memory replay store is for tests and one-process deployments only; production cells sharing an audience need a bounded durable store with atomic first claim. The destination resolver may return an HTTP origin, service binding, RPC stub, or function reference, and the dispatcher must adapt the request for that target type; destination details are never serialized into a public response.\n\nOn an authenticated, guaranteed pre-execution `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH`, the gateway invalidates its cache, re-resolves placement, and retries exactly once. It does not replay ambiguous timeouts or responses produced after execution began. Existing mutation IDs and idempotency keys remain authoritative for application-level retries.\n\n## Errors and availability\n\n| Code | Meaning | Default behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_NOT_FOUND` | No active placement or a tombstone | Fail closed; do not choose a default region |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_UNAVAILABLE` | Directory read failed | Retryable 503; a miss is not inferred |\n| `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH` | Wrong cell or stale epoch before execution | Internal metadata may trigger one gateway retry |\n| `DATAFN_NAMESPACE_MOVING` | The move fence is active | Retryable 409; no cell accepts new work |\n| `DATAFN_CELL_UNAVAILABLE` | Destination resolution or dispatch failed | Pre-send resolution failures are retryable; ambiguous dispatch failures report `executionStarted: true` and are not retry-safe |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_ASSERTION_INVALID` | Spoofed, expired, or mismatched assertion | Non-retryable 401 |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_RETRY_EXHAUSTED` | The bounded re-resolution was insufficient | Retryable 409; operator review may be required |\n| `DATAFN_PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` | The gateway body limit was exceeded | Non-retryable 413 before dispatch |\n\nOnly a verified internal assertion receives region/epoch hints. Public direct responses do not reveal internal topology.\n\n## WebSocket and sync epochs\n\nUse `server.websocketHandler.addRoutedClient(client, authContext, handshakeRequest)` in placement mode. Routed clients must expose a transport-level `close` operation so fencing can deliver code `4510` before removing the session. The handshake pins namespace, region, and epoch. The legacy synchronous `addClient` rejects connections while placement mode is enabled so an adapter cannot accidentally bypass the fence.\n\nWhen a move starts, call `fenceNamespace(namespace)` (or provide the new minimum epoch) on the source cell. Each fence advances a namespace generation, so a handshake that was still awaiting placement validation cannot register after the fence completes. Stale sessions close with code `4510` and reconnect through the canonical `wsUrl`. Mutation IDs, pull cursors, and reconcile checkpoints remain valid only if the migration copy/validation hooks preserve them; otherwise the application must fail explicitly and require a fresh clone.\n\n## Migration protocol\n\n`migrateDatafnNamespace` implements the minimal fenced order:\n\n1. CAS the active placement to `moving` at a newer epoch.\n2. Quiesce source writes and close/fence old sessions.\n3. Drain permission-directory outbox work.\n4. Copy and validate tenant data, cursors, mutation IDs, and checkpoints.\n5. Rebuild the permission projection and warm the target.\n6. CAS the moving record to a target `active` placement with explicit `resume-target` recovery state at another epoch.\n7. Resume target writes, then clear the recovery state through one final epoch CAS.\n\nFailure before activation CASes to an active source placement with explicit `resume-source` recovery state. Gateway and cell traffic remain fenced until the required `rollbackSource` hook succeeds and a final epoch CAS clears that state; `migrateDatafnNamespace` rejects configurations without that hook before it fences writes. A failed or interrupted rollback can therefore be claimed and retried without publishing a quiesced source. The source rollback hook runs only if the first rollback CAS succeeds. A durable placement directory is required to persist the recovery owner, monotonic `recoveryFence`, and expiry lease across crashes; do not use the in-memory directory for migrations that must survive process restarts. After a crash, one retry claims the expired lease through CAS before continuing. Every external hook boundary must atomically compare and persist `recoveryFence` in the affected system, rejecting lower tokens before applying an effect; timer renewal alone cannot fence an already-running hook after takeover. `recoveryLeaseExpiresAt` is a live getter that reflects successful renewals, but it is scheduling guidance rather than a substitute for the fencing token. Hooks must remain idempotent. `resume-source` and `resume-target` placements remain fenced from gateway and cell traffic until their resume hook succeeds and the final CAS clears recovery state. Set `recoveryLeaseMs` longer than the maximum expected hook interval. A same-region destination-only change is rejected instead of silently ignoring it. Rollback after activation is another explicit migration; never edit a cache, region map, or DNS record out of band.\n\n## Observability and operations\n\nProvide `onEvent` to collect placement lookup/cache hit, forward, mismatch, retry, moving, directory unavailable, destination unavailable, epoch conflict, and assertion failure events. Hash namespace identifiers before sending them to shared telemetry. Monitor directory availability and latency, cross-region dispatch latency, retry exhaustion, assertion failures, permission-outbox backlog, and migration stalls.\n\nRun shadow placement checks before enforcement, enable gateway routing for test tenants, and expand gradually. Keep the original single-region configuration as rollback only until the first placement move; after that, rollback must use the epoch protocol.\n---\ntitle: Canonical-gateway multi-region\ndescription: One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n---\n\n# Canonical-gateway multi-region\n\nUse this mode when every browser, OAuth provider, native app, and API client must see one authority such as `https://account.example.com`, while identity state remains pinned to a regional cell.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe gateway, placement directory, cell registry, dispatcher, and signing-key resolver are server-side infrastructure. Never accept an identity key, region, epoch, cell URL, or internal routing header as a trusted client hint.\n\nThe gateway:\n\n1. Classifies the route and derives a stable identity key from trusted request material. Email/password and OTP flows may use a normalized, keyed email digest; session, refresh, revoke, API-key, OAuth-callback, handoff, deletion, and email-change flows should use a verified opaque routing handle.\n2. Reads or atomically claims `{ identityKey, regionId, epoch, state }` in the placement directory.\n3. Resolves the region through a private cell registry. The registry owns the destination handle; placement never contains a URL or service binding.\n4. Removes every incoming `x-authfn-routing-*` header and creates a short-lived HMAC assertion bound to identity, region, epoch, request id, method, path and query, audience, nonce, and request-body digest.\n5. Dispatches internally. A cell verifies the assertion, atomically claims its nonce, and rereads placement before rate limiting, authentication hooks, database reads/writes, OTP delivery, token issuance, or any other auth side effect.\n\nThe cell returns a signed mismatch only when it can prove `executionStarted: false`. The gateway refreshes placement and retries exactly once. Other failures, an unsigned mismatch, a second mismatch, or any response after execution began are returned without retry.\n\n## TypeScript setup\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  authFnMultiRegionEnvironment,\n  authFnMultiRegionPlugin,\n  createAuthFnCanonicalGateway,\n  createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore,\n} from '@authfn/multi-region';\nimport { authFnPlugins, authfn } from 'authfn';\nimport { createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory } from '@authfn/lookup-dynamodb';\nimport type { Adapter } from '@superfunctions/db';\n\nconst placementWriterRegion = process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_WRITER_REGION!;\nconst placementDirectory = createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory({\n  tableName: process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_TABLE!,\n  consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong',\n  writerRegion: placementWriterRegion,\n  region: placementWriterRegion,\n});\n\nconst keyring = {\n  active: {\n    keyId: process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!,\n    secret: await secrets.resolve(process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!),\n  },\n  previous: await secrets.resolveVerificationKeys(),\n};\n\n// Configure this independently in each regional cell.\nconst environment = authFnMultiRegionEnvironment({\n  regions,\n  routing: {\n    mode: 'gateway',\n    publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n    canonicalCookie: { prefix: 'authfn', domain: '.example.com', sameSite: 'lax' },\n    canonicalOAuth: oauthConfig,\n    placementDirectory,\n    identityKeyForIdentifier: identityKeys.fromEmail,\n    identityKeyForUserId: identityKeys.fromUserId,\n    cell: {\n      regionId: process.env.REGION_ID!,\n      audience: `authfn-cell:${process.env.REGION_ID}`,\n      keyring,\n      replayStore: createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore(), // use an atomic shared store per cell in production\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nexport function createRegionalCell(database: Adapter) {\n  return authfn({\n    plugins: authFnPlugins(authFnMultiRegionPlugin()),\n  }).createServer({ database, environment });\n}\n\nconst gateway = createAuthFnCanonicalGateway({\n  publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n  basePath: '/auth',\n  placementDirectory,\n  keyring,\n  resolveIdentity: resolveTrustedIdentity, // set allowInitialPlacement only for approved first-use routes\n  selectInitialRegion: placementPolicy.select,\n  resolveCell: cellRegistry.resolve, // { regionId, audience, target: opaqueBinding }\n  dispatch: (target, request) => internalTransport.fetch(target, request),\n  handleGlobal: globalAuthMetadata.handle,\n});\n```\n\nThe internal dispatcher must preserve cookies, CSRF headers, and the signed `x-request-id`. Normalize `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For`, and equivalent client-IP headers at the canonical ingress according to one documented trusted-proxy policy; do not accept an arbitrary client-provided forwarding chain.\n\nAn AuthFn runtime configured for gateway mode without `routing.cell` is gateway-only: discovery and the non-enumerating lookup route may execute there, while every identity-scoped route fails closed. Regional runtimes add `routing.cell` and are reachable only through a valid signed assertion.\n\n`createCloudflareIdentityPlacementDirectory` provides the same placement contract over a key-named Durable Object. The DynamoDB convenience adapter deliberately requires `consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong'` and forces consistent reads: every cell and gateway must address the same writer region. Local DynamoDB Global Table replicas are eventually consistent with one another and do not, by themselves, provide globally atomic first claims or epoch moves. Global Tables may replicate the directory for recovery or shadow reads, but routing decisions must not use a local replica until a stronger coordinator establishes ownership. `createStoreBackedAuthFnPlacementDirectory` composes placement over another `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` only when that adapter implements atomic `compareAndSet` across every writer.\n\nPython exposes the equivalent `CanonicalGateway`, `CanonicalRoutingConfig`, `InMemoryIdentityPlacementDirectory`, `InMemoryRoutingReplayStore`, `RoutingKeyring`, `create_cell_routing_middleware`, and `move_identity_placement` contracts from `authfn.plugins.gateway_routing`.\n\n## Route ownership matrix\n\n| Route family | Gateway action | Identity source | Cell behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Discovery and `/regions/lookup` | Terminate globally with the canonical authority and no existence/region signal. | None | Not consulted. |\n| Password and OTP start/verify/reset | Route. Atomically claim on first use if policy allows. | Normalized keyed identifier derived from the body. | Validate before lookup, challenge creation, delivery, verification, or session issue. |\n| Session read/list/revoke/sign-out | Route. | Verified opaque session routing handle. | Validate before session reads, last-seen updates, or revocation. |\n| API-key create/list/revoke/authenticate | Route. | Verified session handle or API-key routing prefix/MAC. | Validate before key lookup or mutation. |\n| Social OAuth start, GET/POST callback, disconnect, and native Apple start/complete | Route. | Verified session, OAuth state, or native handshake routing handle. | Keep issuer and callback canonical; validate before state consumption or identity mutation. |\n| Native/web handoff | Route. | Signed handoff routing handle. | Validate before code creation or exchange. |\n| Account deletion and email change | Route. | Verified session handle for self-service; `identityKeyForUserId` for an administrator's target, independent of whether the admin authorizer supplies an `actorId`. | Validate routing before mutation. Account deletion CAS-fences `active` to durable `deleting` before the cascade, restores `active` if the cascade fails, and publishes `tombstoned` only after success. A second deletion attempt is rejected while that fence exists, preventing concurrent cascades from restoring or finalizing each other's state. After inspecting the cascade outcome, an operator may repair a stranded fence with `restoreAuthFnIdentityDeletion` or `finalizeAuthFnIdentityDeletion`; email-change flows must update placement with an application-level transaction/outbox protocol. |\n\nDo not fall back to a default cell for an established identity whose placement is absent. Initial placement is only for explicitly classified first-use flows. Public lookup responses must be identical for present and absent identities.\n\n## Migration and backfill\n\nBackfill existing users by deriving the same stable identity key used at the gateway and calling `putIfAbsent` with the authoritative current region at epoch 1. Treat a conflicting existing record as an incident; never overwrite it with an unconditional write.\n\n`moveAuthFnIdentityPlacement` performs the minimum fenced sequence:\n\n1. CAS `active(source, epoch N)` to `moving(source → target, N+1)`.\n2. Quiesce source writes and drain in-flight auth/delivery work.\n3. Copy identity state and dependent credentials/tokens, validate it, and warm the target.\n4. Resume target capacity and complete readiness checks while public identity traffic remains fenced by `moving`, then CAS to `active(target, N+2)`. Publishing `active` before the target is fully ready would expose an unroutable owner.\n5. Before activation, failures leave placement fenced as `moving` unless a source recovery callback is supplied. With source recovery, resume the source first and only then CAS to `active(source, N+2)`; a failed source resume must never publish an active source. After target activation, ownership remains at the target.\n\nInvalidate gateway placement caches after every claim, move, tombstone, and operator repair. Old assertions are rejected by epoch even when their TTL has not elapsed.\n\n## Availability and fallback\n\n- Placement directory unavailable: fail closed with `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_DIRECTORY_UNAVAILABLE`; do not guess a region.\n- Placement `moving`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; do not start auth side effects.\n- Placement `deleting`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; the durable deletion fence must be finalized or restored before identity traffic resumes.\n- Missing/tombstoned established placement: fail closed. Do not enumerate the identity through the public lookup route.\n- Cell missing or internal dispatch failure: `AUTHFN_ROUTING_CELL_UNAVAILABLE`.\n- Gateway outage: restore the canonical gateway. Do not expose regional authorities as an automatic fallback because that changes issuer, callback, cookie, and topology contracts.\n- Direct mode remains a separately configured compatibility mode for existing regional clients.\n\n## Key rotation and secret handling\n\nKeep routing keys in a KMS/HSM-backed secret resolver. Never place raw keys, assertions, identity keys, OTPs, cookies, authorization codes, or token bodies in logs. Rotation is add → sign → retire:\n\n1. Add the new key to every cell's verification keyring.\n2. Switch the gateway's `active.keyId` and signer.\n3. Wait longer than assertion TTL plus clock skew and deployment propagation.\n4. Remove the retired verification key.\n\nEvery cell uses an atomic replay store. The in-memory implementation is for tests or a single process; multi-instance cells need shared nonce claims with TTL.\n\n## SLOs, dashboards, and drills\n\nTrack placement lookup latency/availability, claim/CAS conflicts, cache hit rate, dispatch latency by cell, signed mismatch rate, retry success/exhaustion, assertion rejection reason, replay rejection, moving duration, and cell-unavailable errors. Suggested starting objectives are 99.99% directory availability, p99 placement lookup under 50 ms, and fewer than 0.1% routed requests requiring the stale-placement retry; tune these to your infrastructure.\n\nRun these drills before rollout and quarterly afterward:\n\n- deny directory reads and confirm no regional handler or delivery provider runs;\n- remove a cell binding and confirm the error contains no internal target;\n- replay and body-modify a captured assertion and confirm rejection;\n- move an identity during OTP, session refresh, OAuth callback, and handoff flows;\n- fail each migration callback before activation and verify placement stays fenced unless source recovery succeeds;\n- fail target resume and source resume independently, and verify neither region is published active before it is ready;\n- rotate the signing key with mixed-version gateway/cell deployments;\n- verify discovery issuer, OAuth redirect URI, cookie names/domain, browser origins, and native return paths remain canonical before and after a move.\n\n## Rollout\n\nStart with shadow placement reads and mismatch metrics, backfill deterministically, enable signed validation in cells, then send a small canonical-gateway cohort. Increase traffic only after retry exhaustion, assertion rejection, public topology leakage, and issuer/cookie/callback invariants are clean. Roll back gateway traffic routing without deleting placement or disabling cell validation; a later retry can resume from the same epochs.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":11458},"formatted_output":"---\ntitle: Multi-region gateway routing\ndescription: Tenant placement, regional cell fencing, canonical ingress, safe retries, and namespace migration.\n---\n\n# Multi-region gateway routing\n\nDataFn supports an opt-in canonical-gateway model for applications that keep each tenant namespace in one writable regional cell. Clients keep one `remote` and `wsUrl`; regional destinations remain internal deployment handles.\n\nThis capability does not provide active-active writes or automatic failover to an empty region. Existing single-region configurations and permission-directory grant indexing are unchanged unless a placement runtime is configured.\n\n## Separate ownership from permission projections\n\n`DatafnPlacementDirectoryAdapter` is the authority for namespace ownership. Its records contain a namespace, owning `regionId`, monotonically increasing `epoch`, lifecycle state, update time, and optional cache/destination metadata. `get`, `putIfAbsent`, and `compareAndSet` must all be linearizable for a namespace; for DynamoDB-backed atomic KV stores, configure strong reads (`consistentRead: true`).\n\nThe indexed permission directory is a separate projection. Permission-directory lag can affect sharing discovery, but it never changes a namespace's database owner. Migration must drain its outbox and rebuild the target projection explicitly.\n\nThe built-in `createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory` composes the focused placement contract with any `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` that implements `compareAndSet`. Its required `consistencyModel: \"linearizable\"` acknowledgement makes the deployment contract explicit: DynamoDB adapters must enable `consistentRead: true`, and other providers must route reads and CAS writes through one linearizable authority. Memory, Redis, and Cloudflare Durable Object stores can use the same contract without placing provider APIs in DataFn core.\n\n`createMemoryDatafnPlacementDirectory` is for tests and one-process deployments only. Migrations that must recover after a process crash require a durable placement directory so recovery ownership, fencing tokens, and lease state survive the restart.\n\n## Regional cell\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory,\n  createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions,\n  createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore,\n  createDatafnServer,\n  datafnMultiRegionPlugin,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst assertions = createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions({\n  activeKeyId: \"2026-08\",\n  keys: { \"2026-08\": env.DATAFN_ROUTING_KEY },\n});\nconst replayStore = createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore(); // tests and one-process deployments only\n\nconst placement = createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory(\n  stores.atomicKv,\n  { consistencyModel: \"linearizable\" },\n);\n\nconst server = await createDatafnServer({\n  schema,\n  database: regionalDatabase,\n  context: authenticateRequest,\n  namespaceProvider: {\n    getNamespace: (context) => context.authorizedNamespace,\n  },\n  plugins: [\n    datafnMultiRegionPlugin({\n      regionId: \"eu-west\",\n      directory: permissionDirectory,\n      placement: {\n        directory: placement,\n        requireRoutingAssertion: true,\n        assertionVerifier: assertions,\n        replayStore,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nThe server derives the trusted namespace from authenticated context, verifies the gateway assertion, and checks region + epoch before plugin authorization, application authorization, or a database/sync operation. The built-in payload containment guard may reject internal-resource references before this placement check; it does not perform route or application authorization. Built-in HTTP, REST, sync, in-process search, public-link routes, and routed WebSocket handshakes use this fence. Health status remains cell-local and does not require a tenant placement.\n\nCustom plugin routes must declare `meta.datafnPlacement.resolveNamespace`. The resolver either returns the authenticated namespace or an early `Response`. An undeclared custom route is rejected at startup in placement mode. Routes that are proven global and do not touch regional data may declare `datafnPlacement: \"none\"`.\n\n## Canonical gateway\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createDatafnGatewayRouter,\n  withDatafnRoutingAssertion,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst gateway = createDatafnGatewayRouter({\n  directory: placement,\n  deriveNamespace: async (request) =>\n    (await authenticateRequest(request)).authorizedNamespace,\n  cellRegistry: {\n    resolve: ({ regionId, destinationRef }) =>\n      internalCells.resolve(regionId, destinationRef),\n  },\n  assertionSigner: assertions,\n  dispatcher: {\n    // HTTP-origin example. Adapt this call when resolve() returns an RPC stub,\n    // service binding, or function reference instead of a fetch-capable target.\n    dispatch: ({ target, request, assertion }) =>\n      target.fetch(withDatafnRoutingAssertion(request, assertion)),\n  },\n  maxCacheEntries: 10_000,\n});\n```\n\nThe gateway strips caller-supplied internal headers, preserves the method, complete path and query string, body, cookies, authorization, correlation/idempotency headers, and request ID, then signs namespace, region, epoch, method/request target, body presence and digest, audience, timestamps, and a nonce. It buffers and hashes at most 5 MiB before invoking even a body-reading namespace resolver, so resolver clones cannot accumulate an unbounded stream; set `maxBodyBytes` on the gateway and placement runtime when a deployment uses another limit. The placement cache also has an insertion-ordered bound (`maxCacheEntries`, default 10,000) in addition to its TTL. Each cell atomically consumes the signed nonce through a shared replay store before execution. Assertion-enabled placement configuration fails at startup unless it supplies both a verifier and replay store. The in-memory replay store is for tests and one-process deployments only; production cells sharing an audience need a bounded durable store with atomic first claim. The destination resolver may return an HTTP origin, service binding, RPC stub, or function reference, and the dispatcher must adapt the request for that target type; destination details are never serialized into a public response.\n\nOn an authenticated, guaranteed pre-execution `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH`, the gateway invalidates its cache, re-resolves placement, and retries exactly once. It does not replay ambiguous timeouts or responses produced after execution began. Existing mutation IDs and idempotency keys remain authoritative for application-level retries.\n\n## Errors and availability\n\n| Code | Meaning | Default behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_NOT_FOUND` | No active placement or a tombstone | Fail closed; do not choose a default region |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_UNAVAILABLE` | Directory read failed | Retryable 503; a miss is not inferred |\n| `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH` | Wrong cell or stale epoch before execution | Internal metadata may trigger one gateway retry |\n| `DATAFN_NAMESPACE_MOVING` | The move fence is active | Retryable 409; no cell accepts new work |\n| `DATAFN_CELL_UNAVAILABLE` | Destination resolution or dispatch failed | Pre-send resolution failures are retryable; ambiguous dispatch failures report `executionStarted: true` and are not retry-safe |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_ASSERTION_INVALID` | Spoofed, expired, or mismatched assertion | Non-retryable 401 |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_RETRY_EXHAUSTED` | The bounded re-resolution was insufficient | Retryable 409; operator review may be required |\n| `DATAFN_PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` | The gateway body limit was exceeded | Non-retryable 413 before dispatch |\n\nOnly a verified internal assertion receives region/epoch hints. Public direct responses do not reveal internal topology.\n\n## WebSocket and sync epochs\n\nUse `server.websocketHandler.addRoutedClient(client, authContext, handshakeRequest)` in placement mode. Routed clients must expose a transport-level `close` operation so fencing can deliver code `4510` before removing the session. The handshake pins namespace, region, and epoch. The legacy synchronous `addClient` rejects connections while placement mode is enabled so an adapter cannot accidentally bypass the fence.\n\nWhen a move starts, call `fenceNamespace(namespace)` (or provide the new minimum epoch) on the source cell. Each fence advances a namespace generation, so a handshake that was still awaiting placement validation cannot register after the fence completes. Stale sessions close with code `4510` and reconnect through the canonical `wsUrl`. Mutation IDs, pull cursors, and reconcile checkpoints remain valid only if the migration copy/validation hooks preserve them; otherwise the application must fail explicitly and require a fresh clone.\n\n## Migration protocol\n\n`migrateDatafnNamespace` implements the minimal fenced order:\n\n1. CAS the active placement to `moving` at a newer epoch.\n2. Quiesce source writes and close/fence old sessions.\n3. Drain permission-directory outbox work.\n4. Copy and validate tenant data, cursors, mutation IDs, and checkpoints.\n5. Rebuild the permission projection and warm the target.\n6. CAS the moving record to a target `active` placement with explicit `resume-target` recovery state at another epoch.\n7. Resume target writes, then clear the recovery state through one final epoch CAS.\n\nFailure before activation CASes to an active source placement with explicit `resume-source` recovery state. Gateway and cell traffic remain fenced until the required `rollbackSource` hook succeeds and a final epoch CAS clears that state; `migrateDatafnNamespace` rejects configurations without that hook before it fences writes. A failed or interrupted rollback can therefore be claimed and retried without publishing a quiesced source. The source rollback hook runs only if the first rollback CAS succeeds. A durable placement directory is required to persist the recovery owner, monotonic `recoveryFence`, and expiry lease across crashes; do not use the in-memory directory for migrations that must survive process restarts. After a crash, one retry claims the expired lease through CAS before continuing. Every external hook boundary must atomically compare and persist `recoveryFence` in the affected system, rejecting lower tokens before applying an effect; timer renewal alone cannot fence an already-running hook after takeover. `recoveryLeaseExpiresAt` is a live getter that reflects successful renewals, but it is scheduling guidance rather than a substitute for the fencing token. Hooks must remain idempotent. `resume-source` and `resume-target` placements remain fenced from gateway and cell traffic until their resume hook succeeds and the final CAS clears recovery state. Set `recoveryLeaseMs` longer than the maximum expected hook interval. A same-region destination-only change is rejected instead of silently ignoring it. Rollback after activation is another explicit migration; never edit a cache, region map, or DNS record out of band.\n\n## Observability and operations\n\nProvide `onEvent` to collect placement lookup/cache hit, forward, mismatch, retry, moving, directory unavailable, destination unavailable, epoch conflict, and assertion failure events. Hash namespace identifiers before sending them to shared telemetry. Monitor directory availability and latency, cross-region dispatch latency, retry exhaustion, assertion failures, permission-outbox backlog, and migration stalls.\n\nRun shadow placement checks before enforcement, enable gateway routing for test tenants, and expand gradually. Keep the original single-region configuration as rollback only until the first placement move; after that, rollback must use the epoch protocol.\n---\ntitle: Canonical-gateway multi-region\ndescription: One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n---\n\n# Canonical-gateway multi-region\n\nUse this mode when every browser, OAuth provider, native app, and API client must see one authority such as `https://account.example.com`, while identity state remains pinned to a regional cell.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe gateway, placement directory, cell registry, dispatcher, and signing-key resolver are server-side infrastructure. Never accept an identity key, region, epoch, cell URL, or internal routing header as a trusted client hint.\n\nThe gateway:\n\n1. Classifies the route and derives a stable identity key from trusted request material. Email/password and OTP flows may use a normalized, keyed email digest; session, refresh, revoke, API-key, OAuth-callback, handoff, deletion, and email-change flows should use a verified opaque routing handle.\n2. Reads or atomically claims `{ identityKey, regionId, epoch, state }` in the placement directory.\n3. Resolves the region through a private cell registry. The registry owns the destination handle; placement never contains a URL or service binding.\n4. Removes every incoming `x-authfn-routing-*` header and creates a short-lived HMAC assertion bound to identity, region, epoch, request id, method, path and query, audience, nonce, and request-body digest.\n5. Dispatches internally. A cell verifies the assertion, atomically claims its nonce, and rereads placement before rate limiting, authentication hooks, database reads/writes, OTP delivery, token issuance, or any other auth side effect.\n\nThe cell returns a signed mismatch only when it can prove `executionStarted: false`. The gateway refreshes placement and retries exactly once. Other failures, an unsigned mismatch, a second mismatch, or any response after execution began are returned without retry.\n\n## TypeScript setup\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  authFnMultiRegionEnvironment,\n  authFnMultiRegionPlugin,\n  createAuthFnCanonicalGateway,\n  createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore,\n} from '@authfn/multi-region';\nimport { authFnPlugins, authfn } from 'authfn';\nimport { createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory } from '@authfn/lookup-dynamodb';\nimport type { Adapter } from '@superfunctions/db';\n\nconst placementWriterRegion = process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_WRITER_REGION!;\nconst placementDirectory = createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory({\n  tableName: process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_TABLE!,\n  consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong',\n  writerRegion: placementWriterRegion,\n  region: placementWriterRegion,\n});\n\nconst keyring = {\n  active: {\n    keyId: process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!,\n    secret: await secrets.resolve(process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!),\n  },\n  previous: await secrets.resolveVerificationKeys(),\n};\n\n// Configure this independently in each regional cell.\nconst environment = authFnMultiRegionEnvironment({\n  regions,\n  routing: {\n    mode: 'gateway',\n    publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n    canonicalCookie: { prefix: 'authfn', domain: '.example.com', sameSite: 'lax' },\n    canonicalOAuth: oauthConfig,\n    placementDirectory,\n    identityKeyForIdentifier: identityKeys.fromEmail,\n    identityKeyForUserId: identityKeys.fromUserId,\n    cell: {\n      regionId: process.env.REGION_ID!,\n      audience: `authfn-cell:${process.env.REGION_ID}`,\n      keyring,\n      replayStore: createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore(), // use an atomic shared store per cell in production\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nexport function createRegionalCell(database: Adapter) {\n  return authfn({\n    plugins: authFnPlugins(authFnMultiRegionPlugin()),\n  }).createServer({ database, environment });\n}\n\nconst gateway = createAuthFnCanonicalGateway({\n  publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n  basePath: '/auth',\n  placementDirectory,\n  keyring,\n  resolveIdentity: resolveTrustedIdentity, // set allowInitialPlacement only for approved first-use routes\n  selectInitialRegion: placementPolicy.select,\n  resolveCell: cellRegistry.resolve, // { regionId, audience, target: opaqueBinding }\n  dispatch: (target, request) => internalTransport.fetch(target, request),\n  handleGlobal: globalAuthMetadata.handle,\n});\n```\n\nThe internal dispatcher must preserve cookies, CSRF headers, and the signed `x-request-id`. Normalize `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For`, and equivalent client-IP headers at the canonical ingress according to one documented trusted-proxy policy; do not accept an arbitrary client-provided forwarding chain.\n\nAn AuthFn runtime configured for gateway mode without `routing.cell` is gateway-only: discovery and the non-enumerating lookup route may execute there, while every identity-scoped route fails closed. Regional runtimes add `routing.cell` and are reachable only through a valid signed assertion.\n\n`createCloudflareIdentityPlacementDirectory` provides the same placement contract over a key-named Durable Object. The DynamoDB convenience adapter deliberately requires `consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong'` and forces consistent reads: every cell and gateway must address the same writer region. Local DynamoDB Global Table replicas are eventually consistent with one another and do not, by themselves, provide globally atomic first claims or epoch moves. Global Tables may replicate the directory for recovery or shadow reads, but routing decisions must not use a local replica until a stronger coordinator establishes ownership. `createStoreBackedAuthFnPlacementDirectory` composes placement over another `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` only when that adapter implements atomic `compareAndSet` across every writer.\n\nPython exposes the equivalent `CanonicalGateway`, `CanonicalRoutingConfig`, `InMemoryIdentityPlacementDirectory`, `InMemoryRoutingReplayStore`, `RoutingKeyring`, `create_cell_routing_middleware`, and `move_identity_placement` contracts from `authfn.plugins.gateway_routing`.\n\n## Route ownership matrix\n\n| Route family | Gateway action | Identity source | Cell behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Discovery and `/regions/lookup` | Terminate globally with the canonical authority and no existence/region signal. | None | Not consulted. |\n| Password and OTP start/verify/reset | Route. Atomically claim on first use if policy allows. | Normalized keyed identifier derived from the body. | Validate before lookup, challenge creation, delivery, verification, or session issue. |\n| Session read/list/revoke/sign-out | Route. | Verified opaque session routing handle. | Validate before session reads, last-seen updates, or revocation. |\n| API-key create/list/revoke/authenticate | Route. | Verified session handle or API-key routing prefix/MAC. | Validate before key lookup or mutation. |\n| Social OAuth start, GET/POST callback, disconnect, and native Apple start/complete | Route. | Verified session, OAuth state, or native handshake routing handle. | Keep issuer and callback canonical; validate before state consumption or identity mutation. |\n| Native/web handoff | Route. | Signed handoff routing handle. | Validate before code creation or exchange. |\n| Account deletion and email change | Route. | Verified session handle for self-service; `identityKeyForUserId` for an administrator's target, independent of whether the admin authorizer supplies an `actorId`. | Validate routing before mutation. Account deletion CAS-fences `active` to durable `deleting` before the cascade, restores `active` if the cascade fails, and publishes `tombstoned` only after success. A second deletion attempt is rejected while that fence exists, preventing concurrent cascades from restoring or finalizing each other's state. After inspecting the cascade outcome, an operator may repair a stranded fence with `restoreAuthFnIdentityDeletion` or `finalizeAuthFnIdentityDeletion`; email-change flows must update placement with an application-level transaction/outbox protocol. |\n\nDo not fall back to a default cell for an established identity whose placement is absent. Initial placement is only for explicitly classified first-use flows. Public lookup responses must be identical for present and absent identities.\n\n## Migration and backfill\n\nBackfill existing users by deriving the same stable identity key used at the gateway and calling `putIfAbsent` with the authoritative current region at epoch 1. Treat a conflicting existing record as an incident; never overwrite it with an unconditional write.\n\n`moveAuthFnIdentityPlacement` performs the minimum fenced sequence:\n\n1. CAS `active(source, epoch N)` to `moving(source → target, N+1)`.\n2. Quiesce source writes and drain in-flight auth/delivery work.\n3. Copy identity state and dependent credentials/tokens, validate it, and warm the target.\n4. Resume target capacity and complete readiness checks while public identity traffic remains fenced by `moving`, then CAS to `active(target, N+2)`. Publishing `active` before the target is fully ready would expose an unroutable owner.\n5. Before activation, failures leave placement fenced as `moving` unless a source recovery callback is supplied. With source recovery, resume the source first and only then CAS to `active(source, N+2)`; a failed source resume must never publish an active source. After target activation, ownership remains at the target.\n\nInvalidate gateway placement caches after every claim, move, tombstone, and operator repair. Old assertions are rejected by epoch even when their TTL has not elapsed.\n\n## Availability and fallback\n\n- Placement directory unavailable: fail closed with `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_DIRECTORY_UNAVAILABLE`; do not guess a region.\n- Placement `moving`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; do not start auth side effects.\n- Placement `deleting`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; the durable deletion fence must be finalized or restored before identity traffic resumes.\n- Missing/tombstoned established placement: fail closed. Do not enumerate the identity through the public lookup route.\n- Cell missing or internal dispatch failure: `AUTHFN_ROUTING_CELL_UNAVAILABLE`.\n- Gateway outage: restore the canonical gateway. Do not expose regional authorities as an automatic fallback because that changes issuer, callback, cookie, and topology contracts.\n- Direct mode remains a separately configured compatibility mode for existing regional clients.\n\n## Key rotation and secret handling\n\nKeep routing keys in a KMS/HSM-backed secret resolver. Never place raw keys, assertions, identity keys, OTPs, cookies, authorization codes, or token bodies in logs. Rotation is add → sign → retire:\n\n1. Add the new key to every cell's verification keyring.\n2. Switch the gateway's `active.keyId` and signer.\n3. Wait longer than assertion TTL plus clock skew and deployment propagation.\n4. Remove the retired verification key.\n\nEvery cell uses an atomic replay store. The in-memory implementation is for tests or a single process; multi-instance cells need shared nonce claims with TTL.\n\n## SLOs, dashboards, and drills\n\nTrack placement lookup latency/availability, claim/CAS conflicts, cache hit rate, dispatch latency by cell, signed mismatch rate, retry success/exhaustion, assertion rejection reason, replay rejection, moving duration, and cell-unavailable errors. Suggested starting objectives are 99.99% directory availability, p99 placement lookup under 50 ms, and fewer than 0.1% routed requests requiring the stale-placement retry; tune these to your infrastructure.\n\nRun these drills before rollout and quarterly afterward:\n\n- deny directory reads and confirm no regional handler or delivery provider runs;\n- remove a cell binding and confirm the error contains no internal target;\n- replay and body-modify a captured assertion and confirm rejection;\n- move an identity during OTP, session refresh, OAuth callback, and handoff flows;\n- fail each migration callback before activation and verify placement stays fenced unless source recovery succeeds;\n- fail target resume and source resume independently, and verify neither region is published active before it is ready;\n- rotate the signing key with mixed-version gateway/cell deployments;\n- verify discovery issuer, OAuth redirect URI, cookie names/domain, browser origins, and native return paths remain canonical before and after a move.\n\n## Rollout\n\nStart with shadow placement reads and mismatch metrics, backfill deterministically, enable signed validation in cells, then send a small canonical-gateway cohort. Increase traffic only after retry exhaustion, assertion rejection, public topology leakage, and issuer/cookie/callback invariants are clean. Roll back gateway traffic routing without deleting placement or disabling cell validation; a later retry can resume from the same epochs.\n"},"started_at_ms":1787984687521,"completed_at_ms":1787984687522}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T06:24:47.814Z","ordinal":12125,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04c31-32c6-70a2-850b-928cf8fe4fe6","call_id":"call_PxEWMqV79eQw1wJnQWHURAru","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"---\ntitle: Multi-region gateway routing\ndescription: Tenant placement, regional cell fencing, canonical ingress, safe retries, and namespace migration.\n---\n\n# Multi-region gateway routing\n\nDataFn supports an opt-in canonical-gateway model for applications that keep each tenant namespace in one writable regional cell. Clients keep one `remote` and `wsUrl`; regional destinations remain internal deployment handles.\n\nThis capability does not provide active-active writes or automatic failover to an empty region. Existing single-region configurations and permission-directory grant indexing are unchanged unless a placement runtime is configured.\n\n## Separate ownership from permission projections\n\n`DatafnPlacementDirectoryAdapter` is the authority for namespace ownership. Its records contain a namespace, owning `regionId`, monotonically increasing `epoch`, lifecycle state, update time, and optional cache/destination metadata. `get`, `putIfAbsent`, and `compareAndSet` must all be linearizable for a namespace; for DynamoDB-backed atomic KV stores, configure strong reads (`consistentRead: true`).\n\nThe indexed permission directory is a separate projection. Permission-directory lag can affect sharing discovery, but it never changes a namespace's database owner. Migration must drain its outbox and rebuild the target projection explicitly.\n\nThe built-in `createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory` composes the focused placement contract with any `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` that implements `compareAndSet`. Its required `consistencyModel: \"linearizable\"` acknowledgement makes the deployment contract explicit: DynamoDB adapters must enable `consistentRead: true`, and other providers must route reads and CAS writes through one linearizable authority. Memory, Redis, and Cloudflare Durable Object stores can use the same contract without placing provider APIs in DataFn core.\n\n`createMemoryDatafnPlacementDirectory` is for tests and one-process deployments only. Migrations that must recover after a process crash require a durable placement directory so recovery ownership, fencing tokens, and lease state survive the restart.\n\n## Regional cell\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory,\n  createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions,\n  createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore,\n  createDatafnServer,\n  datafnMultiRegionPlugin,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst assertions = createDatafnHmacRoutingAssertions({\n  activeKeyId: \"2026-08\",\n  keys: { \"2026-08\": env.DATAFN_ROUTING_KEY },\n});\nconst replayStore = createMemoryDatafnRoutingReplayStore(); // tests and one-process deployments only\n\nconst placement = createConditionalKvDatafnPlacementDirectory(\n  stores.atomicKv,\n  { consistencyModel: \"linearizable\" },\n);\n\nconst server = await createDatafnServer({\n  schema,\n  database: regionalDatabase,\n  context: authenticateRequest,\n  namespaceProvider: {\n    getNamespace: (context) => context.authorizedNamespace,\n  },\n  plugins: [\n    datafnMultiRegionPlugin({\n      regionId: \"eu-west\",\n      directory: permissionDirectory,\n      placement: {\n        directory: placement,\n        requireRoutingAssertion: true,\n        assertionVerifier: assertions,\n        replayStore,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n});\n```\n\nThe server derives the trusted namespace from authenticated context, verifies the gateway assertion, and checks region + epoch before plugin authorization, application authorization, or a database/sync operation. The built-in payload containment guard may reject internal-resource references before this placement check; it does not perform route or application authorization. Built-in HTTP, REST, sync, in-process search, public-link routes, and routed WebSocket handshakes use this fence. Health status remains cell-local and does not require a tenant placement.\n\nCustom plugin routes must declare `meta.datafnPlacement.resolveNamespace`. The resolver either returns the authenticated namespace or an early `Response`. An undeclared custom route is rejected at startup in placement mode. Routes that are proven global and do not touch regional data may declare `datafnPlacement: \"none\"`.\n\n## Canonical gateway\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  createDatafnGatewayRouter,\n  withDatafnRoutingAssertion,\n} from \"@datafn/server\";\n\nconst gateway = createDatafnGatewayRouter({\n  directory: placement,\n  deriveNamespace: async (request) =>\n    (await authenticateRequest(request)).authorizedNamespace,\n  cellRegistry: {\n    resolve: ({ regionId, destinationRef }) =>\n      internalCells.resolve(regionId, destinationRef),\n  },\n  assertionSigner: assertions,\n  dispatcher: {\n    // HTTP-origin example. Adapt this call when resolve() returns an RPC stub,\n    // service binding, or function reference instead of a fetch-capable target.\n    dispatch: ({ target, request, assertion }) =>\n      target.fetch(withDatafnRoutingAssertion(request, assertion)),\n  },\n  maxCacheEntries: 10_000,\n});\n```\n\nThe gateway strips caller-supplied internal headers, preserves the method, complete path and query string, body, cookies, authorization, correlation/idempotency headers, and request ID, then signs namespace, region, epoch, method/request target, body presence and digest, audience, timestamps, and a nonce. It buffers and hashes at most 5 MiB before invoking even a body-reading namespace resolver, so resolver clones cannot accumulate an unbounded stream; set `maxBodyBytes` on the gateway and placement runtime when a deployment uses another limit. The placement cache also has an insertion-ordered bound (`maxCacheEntries`, default 10,000) in addition to its TTL. Each cell atomically consumes the signed nonce through a shared replay store before execution. Assertion-enabled placement configuration fails at startup unless it supplies both a verifier and replay store. The in-memory replay store is for tests and one-process deployments only; production cells sharing an audience need a bounded durable store with atomic first claim. The destination resolver may return an HTTP origin, service binding, RPC stub, or function reference, and the dispatcher must adapt the request for that target type; destination details are never serialized into a public response.\n\nOn an authenticated, guaranteed pre-execution `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH`, the gateway invalidates its cache, re-resolves placement, and retries exactly once. It does not replay ambiguous timeouts or responses produced after execution began. Existing mutation IDs and idempotency keys remain authoritative for application-level retries.\n\n## Errors and availability\n\n| Code | Meaning | Default behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_NOT_FOUND` | No active placement or a tombstone | Fail closed; do not choose a default region |\n| `DATAFN_PLACEMENT_UNAVAILABLE` | Directory read failed | Retryable 503; a miss is not inferred |\n| `DATAFN_REGION_MISMATCH` | Wrong cell or stale epoch before execution | Internal metadata may trigger one gateway retry |\n| `DATAFN_NAMESPACE_MOVING` | The move fence is active | Retryable 409; no cell accepts new work |\n| `DATAFN_CELL_UNAVAILABLE` | Destination resolution or dispatch failed | Pre-send resolution failures are retryable; ambiguous dispatch failures report `executionStarted: true` and are not retry-safe |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_ASSERTION_INVALID` | Spoofed, expired, or mismatched assertion | Non-retryable 401 |\n| `DATAFN_ROUTING_RETRY_EXHAUSTED` | The bounded re-resolution was insufficient | Retryable 409; operator review may be required |\n| `DATAFN_PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` | The gateway body limit was exceeded | Non-retryable 413 before dispatch |\n\nOnly a verified internal assertion receives region/epoch hints. Public direct responses do not reveal internal topology.\n\n## WebSocket and sync epochs\n\nUse `server.websocketHandler.addRoutedClient(client, authContext, handshakeRequest)` in placement mode. Routed clients must expose a transport-level `close` operation so fencing can deliver code `4510` before removing the session. The handshake pins namespace, region, and epoch. The legacy synchronous `addClient` rejects connections while placement mode is enabled so an adapter cannot accidentally bypass the fence.\n\nWhen a move starts, call `fenceNamespace(namespace)` (or provide the new minimum epoch) on the source cell. Each fence advances a namespace generation, so a handshake that was still awaiting placement validation cannot register after the fence completes. Stale sessions close with code `4510` and reconnect through the canonical `wsUrl`. Mutation IDs, pull cursors, and reconcile checkpoints remain valid only if the migration copy/validation hooks preserve them; otherwise the application must fail explicitly and require a fresh clone.\n\n## Migration protocol\n\n`migrateDatafnNamespace` implements the minimal fenced order:\n\n1. CAS the active placement to `moving` at a newer epoch.\n2. Quiesce source writes and close/fence old sessions.\n3. Drain permission-directory outbox work.\n4. Copy and validate tenant data, cursors, mutation IDs, and checkpoints.\n5. Rebuild the permission projection and warm the target.\n6. CAS the moving record to a target `active` placement with explicit `resume-target` recovery state at another epoch.\n7. Resume target writes, then clear the recovery state through one final epoch CAS.\n\nFailure before activation CASes to an active source placement with explicit `resume-source` recovery state. Gateway and cell traffic remain fenced until the required `rollbackSource` hook succeeds and a final epoch CAS clears that state; `migrateDatafnNamespace` rejects configurations without that hook before it fences writes. A failed or interrupted rollback can therefore be claimed and retried without publishing a quiesced source. The source rollback hook runs only if the first rollback CAS succeeds. A durable placement directory is required to persist the recovery owner, monotonic `recoveryFence`, and expiry lease across crashes; do not use the in-memory directory for migrations that must survive process restarts. After a crash, one retry claims the expired lease through CAS before continuing. Every external hook boundary must atomically compare and persist `recoveryFence` in the affected system, rejecting lower tokens before applying an effect; timer renewal alone cannot fence an already-running hook after takeover. `recoveryLeaseExpiresAt` is a live getter that reflects successful renewals, but it is scheduling guidance rather than a substitute for the fencing token. Hooks must remain idempotent. `resume-source` and `resume-target` placements remain fenced from gateway and cell traffic until their resume hook succeeds and the final CAS clears recovery state. Set `recoveryLeaseMs` longer than the maximum expected hook interval. A same-region destination-only change is rejected instead of silently ignoring it. Rollback after activation is another explicit migration; never edit a cache, region map, or DNS record out of band.\n\n## Observability and operations\n\nProvide `onEvent` to collect placement lookup/cache hit, forward, mismatch, retry, moving, directory unavailable, destination unavailable, epoch conflict, and assertion failure events. Hash namespace identifiers before sending them to shared telemetry. Monitor directory availability and latency, cross-region dispatch latency, retry exhaustion, assertion failures, permission-outbox backlog, and migration stalls.\n\nRun shadow placement checks before enforcement, enable gateway routing for test tenants, and expand gradually. Keep the original single-region configuration as rollback only until the first placement move; after that, rollback must use the epoch protocol.\n---\ntitle: Canonical-gateway multi-region\ndescription: One stable AuthFn authority, canonical identity placement, signed cell forwarding, migrations, and an operator runbook.\n---\n\n# Canonical-gateway multi-region\n\nUse this mode when every browser, OAuth provider, native app, and API client must see one authority such as `https://account.example.com`, while identity state remains pinned to a regional cell.\n\n## Trust boundaries\n\nThe gateway, placement directory, cell registry, dispatcher, and signing-key resolver are server-side infrastructure. Never accept an identity key, region, epoch, cell URL, or internal routing header as a trusted client hint.\n\nThe gateway:\n\n1. Classifies the route and derives a stable identity key from trusted request material. Email/password and OTP flows may use a normalized, keyed email digest; session, refresh, revoke, API-key, OAuth-callback, handoff, deletion, and email-change flows should use a verified opaque routing handle.\n2. Reads or atomically claims `{ identityKey, regionId, epoch, state }` in the placement directory.\n3. Resolves the region through a private cell registry. The registry owns the destination handle; placement never contains a URL or service binding.\n4. Removes every incoming `x-authfn-routing-*` header and creates a short-lived HMAC assertion bound to identity, region, epoch, request id, method, path and query, audience, nonce, and request-body digest.\n5. Dispatches internally. A cell verifies the assertion, atomically claims its nonce, and rereads placement before rate limiting, authentication hooks, database reads/writes, OTP delivery, token issuance, or any other auth side effect.\n\nThe cell returns a signed mismatch only when it can prove `executionStarted: false`. The gateway refreshes placement and retries exactly once. Other failures, an unsigned mismatch, a second mismatch, or any response after execution began are returned without retry.\n\n## TypeScript setup\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  authFnMultiRegionEnvironment,\n  authFnMultiRegionPlugin,\n  createAuthFnCanonicalGateway,\n  createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore,\n} from '@authfn/multi-region';\nimport { authFnPlugins, authfn } from 'authfn';\nimport { createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory } from '@authfn/lookup-dynamodb';\nimport type { Adapter } from '@superfunctions/db';\n\nconst placementWriterRegion = process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_WRITER_REGION!;\nconst placementDirectory = createDynamoDbIdentityPlacementDirectory({\n  tableName: process.env.AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_TABLE!,\n  consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong',\n  writerRegion: placementWriterRegion,\n  region: placementWriterRegion,\n});\n\nconst keyring = {\n  active: {\n    keyId: process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!,\n    secret: await secrets.resolve(process.env.AUTHFN_ROUTING_KEY_ID!),\n  },\n  previous: await secrets.resolveVerificationKeys(),\n};\n\n// Configure this independently in each regional cell.\nconst environment = authFnMultiRegionEnvironment({\n  regions,\n  routing: {\n    mode: 'gateway',\n    publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n    canonicalCookie: { prefix: 'authfn', domain: '.example.com', sameSite: 'lax' },\n    canonicalOAuth: oauthConfig,\n    placementDirectory,\n    identityKeyForIdentifier: identityKeys.fromEmail,\n    identityKeyForUserId: identityKeys.fromUserId,\n    cell: {\n      regionId: process.env.REGION_ID!,\n      audience: `authfn-cell:${process.env.REGION_ID}`,\n      keyring,\n      replayStore: createInMemoryAuthFnRoutingReplayStore(), // use an atomic shared store per cell in production\n    },\n  },\n});\n\nexport function createRegionalCell(database: Adapter) {\n  return authfn({\n    plugins: authFnPlugins(authFnMultiRegionPlugin()),\n  }).createServer({ database, environment });\n}\n\nconst gateway = createAuthFnCanonicalGateway({\n  publicAuthority: 'https://account.example.com',\n  basePath: '/auth',\n  placementDirectory,\n  keyring,\n  resolveIdentity: resolveTrustedIdentity, // set allowInitialPlacement only for approved first-use routes\n  selectInitialRegion: placementPolicy.select,\n  resolveCell: cellRegistry.resolve, // { regionId, audience, target: opaqueBinding }\n  dispatch: (target, request) => internalTransport.fetch(target, request),\n  handleGlobal: globalAuthMetadata.handle,\n});\n```\n\nThe internal dispatcher must preserve cookies, CSRF headers, and the signed `x-request-id`. Normalize `Forwarded`, `X-Forwarded-For`, and equivalent client-IP headers at the canonical ingress according to one documented trusted-proxy policy; do not accept an arbitrary client-provided forwarding chain.\n\nAn AuthFn runtime configured for gateway mode without `routing.cell` is gateway-only: discovery and the non-enumerating lookup route may execute there, while every identity-scoped route fails closed. Regional runtimes add `routing.cell` and are reachable only through a valid signed assertion.\n\n`createCloudflareIdentityPlacementDirectory` provides the same placement contract over a key-named Durable Object. The DynamoDB convenience adapter deliberately requires `consistencyModel: 'single-writer-strong'` and forces consistent reads: every cell and gateway must address the same writer region. Local DynamoDB Global Table replicas are eventually consistent with one another and do not, by themselves, provide globally atomic first claims or epoch moves. Global Tables may replicate the directory for recovery or shadow reads, but routing decisions must not use a local replica until a stronger coordinator establishes ownership. `createStoreBackedAuthFnPlacementDirectory` composes placement over another `ConditionalKVStoreAdapter` only when that adapter implements atomic `compareAndSet` across every writer.\n\nPython exposes the equivalent `CanonicalGateway`, `CanonicalRoutingConfig`, `InMemoryIdentityPlacementDirectory`, `InMemoryRoutingReplayStore`, `RoutingKeyring`, `create_cell_routing_middleware`, and `move_identity_placement` contracts from `authfn.plugins.gateway_routing`.\n\n## Route ownership matrix\n\n| Route family | Gateway action | Identity source | Cell behavior |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Discovery and `/regions/lookup` | Terminate globally with the canonical authority and no existence/region signal. | None | Not consulted. |\n| Password and OTP start/verify/reset | Route. Atomically claim on first use if policy allows. | Normalized keyed identifier derived from the body. | Validate before lookup, challenge creation, delivery, verification, or session issue. |\n| Session read/list/revoke/sign-out | Route. | Verified opaque session routing handle. | Validate before session reads, last-seen updates, or revocation. |\n| API-key create/list/revoke/authenticate | Route. | Verified session handle or API-key routing prefix/MAC. | Validate before key lookup or mutation. |\n| Social OAuth start, GET/POST callback, disconnect, and native Apple start/complete | Route. | Verified session, OAuth state, or native handshake routing handle. | Keep issuer and callback canonical; validate before state consumption or identity mutation. |\n| Native/web handoff | Route. | Signed handoff routing handle. | Validate before code creation or exchange. |\n| Account deletion and email change | Route. | Verified session handle for self-service; `identityKeyForUserId` for an administrator's target, independent of whether the admin authorizer supplies an `actorId`. | Validate routing before mutation. Account deletion CAS-fences `active` to durable `deleting` before the cascade, restores `active` if the cascade fails, and publishes `tombstoned` only after success. A second deletion attempt is rejected while that fence exists, preventing concurrent cascades from restoring or finalizing each other's state. After inspecting the cascade outcome, an operator may repair a stranded fence with `restoreAuthFnIdentityDeletion` or `finalizeAuthFnIdentityDeletion`; email-change flows must update placement with an application-level transaction/outbox protocol. |\n\nDo not fall back to a default cell for an established identity whose placement is absent. Initial placement is only for explicitly classified first-use flows. Public lookup responses must be identical for present and absent identities.\n\n## Migration and backfill\n\nBackfill existing users by deriving the same stable identity key used at the gateway and calling `putIfAbsent` with the authoritative current region at epoch 1. Treat a conflicting existing record as an incident; never overwrite it with an unconditional write.\n\n`moveAuthFnIdentityPlacement` performs the minimum fenced sequence:\n\n1. CAS `active(source, epoch N)` to `moving(source → target, N+1)`.\n2. Quiesce source writes and drain in-flight auth/delivery work.\n3. Copy identity state and dependent credentials/tokens, validate it, and warm the target.\n4. Resume target capacity and complete readiness checks while public identity traffic remains fenced by `moving`, then CAS to `active(target, N+2)`. Publishing `active` before the target is fully ready would expose an unroutable owner.\n5. Before activation, failures leave placement fenced as `moving` unless a source recovery callback is supplied. With source recovery, resume the source first and only then CAS to `active(source, N+2)`; a failed source resume must never publish an active source. After target activation, ownership remains at the target.\n\nInvalidate gateway placement caches after every claim, move, tombstone, and operator repair. Old assertions are rejected by epoch even when their TTL has not elapsed.\n\n## Availability and fallback\n\n- Placement directory unavailable: fail closed with `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_DIRECTORY_UNAVAILABLE`; do not guess a region.\n- Placement `moving`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; do not start auth side effects.\n- Placement `deleting`: return `AUTHFN_PLACEMENT_MOVING`; the durable deletion fence must be finalized or restored before identity traffic resumes.\n- Missing/tombstoned established placement: fail closed. Do not enumerate the identity through the public lookup route.\n- Cell missing or internal dispatch failure: `AUTHFN_ROUTING_CELL_UNAVAILABLE`.\n- Gateway outage: restore the canonical gateway. Do not expose regional authorities as an automatic fallback because that changes issuer, callback, cookie, and topology contracts.\n- Direct mode remains a separately configured compatibility mode for existing regional clients.\n\n## Key rotation and secret handling\n\nKeep routing keys in a KMS/HSM-backed secret resolver. Never place raw keys, assertions, identity keys, OTPs, cookies, authorization codes, or token bodies in logs. Rotation is add → sign → retire:\n\n1. Add the new key to every cell's verification keyring.\n2. Switch the gateway's `active.keyId` and signer.\n3. Wait longer than assertion TTL plus clock skew and deployment propagation.\n4. Remove the retired verification key.\n\nEvery cell uses an atomic replay store. The in-memory implementation is for tests or a single process; multi-instance cells need shared nonce claims with TTL.\n\n## SLOs, dashboards, and drills\n\nTrack placement lookup latency/availability, claim/CAS conflicts, cache hit rate, dispatch latency by cell, signed mismatch rate, retry success/exhaustion, assertion rejection reason, replay rejection, moving duration, and cell-unavailable errors. Suggested starting objectives are 99.99% directory availability, p99 placement lookup under 50 ms, and fewer than 0.1% routed requests requiring the stale-placement retry; tune these to your infrastructure.\n\nRun these drills before rollout and quarterly afterward:\n\n- deny directory reads and confirm no regional handler or delivery provider runs;\n- remove a cell binding and confirm the error contains no internal target;\n- replay and body-modify a captured assertion and confirm rejection;\n- move an identity during OTP, session refresh, OAuth callback, and handoff flows;\n- fail each migration callback before activation and verify placement stays fenced unless source recovery succeeds;\n- fail target resume and source resume independently, and verify neither region is published active before it is ready;\n- rotate the signing key with mixed-version gateway/cell deployments;\n- verify discovery issuer, OAuth redirect URI, cookie names/domain, browser origins, and native return paths remain canonical before and after a move.\n\n## Rollout\n\nStart with shadow placement reads and mismatch metrics, backfill deterministically, enable signed validation in cells, then send a small canonical-gateway cohort. Increase traffic only after retry exhaustion, assertion rejection, public topology leakage, and issuer/cookie/callback invariants are clean. Roll back gateway traffic routing without deleting placement or disabling cell validation; a later retry can resume from the same epochs.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04c30-8e87-7781-bbc7-0acb090224d6","create_time":1787984687.814016}}}
