{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:34.924Z","ordinal":12166,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af","turn_id":"01a04d75-82cd-7981-a1ee-9db4f0724d1a","item":{"type":"Reasoning","id":"rs_0eef853664611d0c016a92ce54f65087d0bcf9cb3ca67b996c","summary_text":["**Clarifying multi-region Auth and Data differences**","**Planning simplified multi-region flow explanation**","**Drafting simple multi-region analogy and diagrams**"],"raw_content":[]},"started_at_ms":1788005973039,"completed_at_ms":1788005974924}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:34.929Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"id":"rs_0eef853664611d0c016a92ce54f65087d0bcf9cb3ca67b996c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04d75-82cd-7981-a1ee-9db4f0724d1a"},"summary":[{"text":"**Clarifying multi-region Auth and Data differences**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning simplified multi-region flow explanation**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Drafting simple multi-region analogy and diagrams**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:47.765Z","ordinal":12168,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a02fc0-218b-7e61-a134-da5cc89b60af","turn_id":"01a04d75-82cd-7981-a1ee-9db4f0724d1a","item":{"type":"AgentMessage","id":"msg_0eef853664611d0c016a92ce56dcbc87d0a3a1f3c2b6e57313","content":[{"type":"Text","text":"Yep. Plain-English version:\n\nThink of each region as a separate warehouse. Before, the customer needed to know which warehouse held their stuff. Now, there is one reception desk that looks it up and sends the request to the right warehouse.\n\n## Earlier\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] --> L[Find or remember the user's region]\n    L --> EU[EU server]\n    L --> US[US server]\n    L --> IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe practical problems were:\n\n- The app or client needed to know which regional URL to use.\n- A cached region could become wrong after moving data.\n- Each region was exposed more directly.\n- There was no single gate immediately before execution proving, “this user or tenant still belongs in this region.”\n- For AuthFn, regional URLs could complicate OAuth callbacks, cookies and issuer URLs.\n\n## Now\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] -->|Always the same public URL| G[Gateway]\n    G --> D[Private directory:<br/>who belongs in which region?]\n    D --> G\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| EU[EU server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| US[US server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe gateway is basically a receptionist:\n\n1. You send every request to one stable URL.\n2. The gateway looks up where your data or account lives.\n3. It sends the request internally to that region.\n4. It attaches a short-lived, tamper-proof “permission slip.”\n5. The regional server checks the slip and checks the directory again.\n6. Only then does it touch the database, issue a token, send an OTP, or process a write.\n\nSo even if the gateway had stale information, the wrong region refuses to execute the request.\n\n## What DATA-1 changed\n\nFor DataFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this company/workspace/tenant’s data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAcme workspace → EU region\nGlobex workspace → India region\n```\n\nThis now protects:\n\n- Database reads and writes\n- Sync operations\n- WebSocket connections\n- REST endpoints\n- DataFn plugin routes\n\nWhen moving a tenant:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[EU owns tenant] --> B[Mark tenant as moving]\n    B --> C[Block new writes and close old WebSockets]\n    C --> D[Copy and verify the data]\n    D --> E[India becomes the new owner]\n```\n\nWhile the tenant is marked “moving,” neither region is allowed to accept new work. This prevents both regions from writing at the same time.\n\n## What AUTH-1 changed\n\nFor AuthFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this person’s identity and authentication data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAlice's identity → EU region\nBob's identity → US region\n```\n\nEvery client still uses one address, such as:\n\n```text\nhttps://account.example.com\n```\n\nThat remains true for:\n\n- Login\n- Sessions\n- OTPs\n- API keys\n- OAuth\n- Password reset\n- Account deletion\n\nThe gateway quietly routes the request to the correct region. OAuth URLs, cookies and issuer URLs remain canonical instead of changing by region.\n\nAccount deletion also became safer:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Account active] --> B[Mark account as deleting]\n    B --> C[Perform deletion]\n    C -->|Success| D[Mark permanently deleted]\n    C -->|Failure| A\n```\n\nThis prevents two servers from deleting or restoring the same account concurrently.\n\nThe shortest summary is:\n\n- **Before:** the client helped choose the region.\n- **Now:** the client always uses one URL; the gateway chooses the region.\n- **DATA-1:** routes tenant data safely.\n- **AUTH-1:** routes identity and authentication safely.\n- **During a move:** traffic stops briefly instead of allowing two regions to become owners.\n- **Old behavior still exists:** DataFn’s placement mode is optional, and AuthFn’s direct regional mode remains available."}],"phase":"final_answer"},"started_at_ms":1788005974932,"completed_at_ms":1788005987765}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:47.771Z","ordinal":12169,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"msg_0eef853664611d0c016a92ce56dcbc87d0a3a1f3c2b6e57313","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"Yep. Plain-English version:\n\nThink of each region as a separate warehouse. Before, the customer needed to know which warehouse held their stuff. Now, there is one reception desk that looks it up and sends the request to the right warehouse.\n\n## Earlier\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] --> L[Find or remember the user's region]\n    L --> EU[EU server]\n    L --> US[US server]\n    L --> IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe practical problems were:\n\n- The app or client needed to know which regional URL to use.\n- A cached region could become wrong after moving data.\n- Each region was exposed more directly.\n- There was no single gate immediately before execution proving, “this user or tenant still belongs in this region.”\n- For AuthFn, regional URLs could complicate OAuth callbacks, cookies and issuer URLs.\n\n## Now\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] -->|Always the same public URL| G[Gateway]\n    G --> D[Private directory:<br/>who belongs in which region?]\n    D --> G\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| EU[EU server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| US[US server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe gateway is basically a receptionist:\n\n1. You send every request to one stable URL.\n2. The gateway looks up where your data or account lives.\n3. It sends the request internally to that region.\n4. It attaches a short-lived, tamper-proof “permission slip.”\n5. The regional server checks the slip and checks the directory again.\n6. Only then does it touch the database, issue a token, send an OTP, or process a write.\n\nSo even if the gateway had stale information, the wrong region refuses to execute the request.\n\n## What DATA-1 changed\n\nFor DataFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this company/workspace/tenant’s data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAcme workspace → EU region\nGlobex workspace → India region\n```\n\nThis now protects:\n\n- Database reads and writes\n- Sync operations\n- WebSocket connections\n- REST endpoints\n- DataFn plugin routes\n\nWhen moving a tenant:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[EU owns tenant] --> B[Mark tenant as moving]\n    B --> C[Block new writes and close old WebSockets]\n    C --> D[Copy and verify the data]\n    D --> E[India becomes the new owner]\n```\n\nWhile the tenant is marked “moving,” neither region is allowed to accept new work. This prevents both regions from writing at the same time.\n\n## What AUTH-1 changed\n\nFor AuthFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this person’s identity and authentication data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAlice's identity → EU region\nBob's identity → US region\n```\n\nEvery client still uses one address, such as:\n\n```text\nhttps://account.example.com\n```\n\nThat remains true for:\n\n- Login\n- Sessions\n- OTPs\n- API keys\n- OAuth\n- Password reset\n- Account deletion\n\nThe gateway quietly routes the request to the correct region. OAuth URLs, cookies and issuer URLs remain canonical instead of changing by region.\n\nAccount deletion also became safer:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Account active] --> B[Mark account as deleting]\n    B --> C[Perform deletion]\n    C -->|Success| D[Mark permanently deleted]\n    C -->|Failure| A\n```\n\nThis prevents two servers from deleting or restoring the same account concurrently.\n\nThe shortest summary is:\n\n- **Before:** the client helped choose the region.\n- **Now:** the client always uses one URL; the gateway chooses the region.\n- **DATA-1:** routes tenant data safely.\n- **AUTH-1:** routes identity and authentication safely.\n- **During a move:** traffic stops briefly instead of allowing two regions to become owners.\n- **Old behavior still exists:** DataFn’s placement mode is optional, and AuthFn’s direct regional mode remains available."}],"phase":"final_answer","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04d75-82cd-7981-a1ee-9db4f0724d1a","content_item_kinds":["unknown"]}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:47.840Z","ordinal":12170,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"token_count","info":{"total_token_usage":{"input_tokens":224005,"cached_input_tokens":125440,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2576,"reasoning_output_tokens":1559,"total_tokens":226581},"last_token_usage":{"input_tokens":115522,"cached_input_tokens":108288,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2030,"reasoning_output_tokens":1161,"total_tokens":117552},"model_context_window":258400},"rate_limits":{"limit_id":"codex","limit_name":null,"primary":{"used_percent":44.0,"window_minutes":10080,"resets_at":1788456712},"secondary":null,"credits":{"has_credits":false,"unlimited":false,"balance":"0"},"individual_limit":null,"spend_control_reached":null,"plan_type":"pro","rate_limit_reached_type":null}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:19:47.962Z","ordinal":12171,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"task_complete","turn_id":"01a04d75-82cd-7981-a1ee-9db4f0724d1a","last_agent_message":"Yep. Plain-English version:\n\nThink of each region as a separate warehouse. Before, the customer needed to know which warehouse held their stuff. Now, there is one reception desk that looks it up and sends the request to the right warehouse.\n\n## Earlier\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] --> L[Find or remember the user's region]\n    L --> EU[EU server]\n    L --> US[US server]\n    L --> IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe practical problems were:\n\n- The app or client needed to know which regional URL to use.\n- A cached region could become wrong after moving data.\n- Each region was exposed more directly.\n- There was no single gate immediately before execution proving, “this user or tenant still belongs in this region.”\n- For AuthFn, regional URLs could complicate OAuth callbacks, cookies and issuer URLs.\n\n## Now\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    U[User or app] -->|Always the same public URL| G[Gateway]\n    G --> D[Private directory:<br/>who belongs in which region?]\n    D --> G\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| EU[EU server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| US[US server]\n    G -->|Send internally to the chosen region| IN[India server]\n```\n\nThe gateway is basically a receptionist:\n\n1. You send every request to one stable URL.\n2. The gateway looks up where your data or account lives.\n3. It sends the request internally to that region.\n4. It attaches a short-lived, tamper-proof “permission slip.”\n5. The regional server checks the slip and checks the directory again.\n6. Only then does it touch the database, issue a token, send an OTP, or process a write.\n\nSo even if the gateway had stale information, the wrong region refuses to execute the request.\n\n## What DATA-1 changed\n\nFor DataFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this company/workspace/tenant’s data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAcme workspace → EU region\nGlobex workspace → India region\n```\n\nThis now protects:\n\n- Database reads and writes\n- Sync operations\n- WebSocket connections\n- REST endpoints\n- DataFn plugin routes\n\nWhen moving a tenant:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[EU owns tenant] --> B[Mark tenant as moving]\n    B --> C[Block new writes and close old WebSockets]\n    C --> D[Copy and verify the data]\n    D --> E[India becomes the new owner]\n```\n\nWhile the tenant is marked “moving,” neither region is allowed to accept new work. This prevents both regions from writing at the same time.\n\n## What AUTH-1 changed\n\nFor AuthFn, the directory answers:\n\n> “Which region owns this person’s identity and authentication data?”\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nAlice's identity → EU region\nBob's identity → US region\n```\n\nEvery client still uses one address, such as:\n\n```text\nhttps://account.example.com\n```\n\nThat remains true for:\n\n- Login\n- Sessions\n- OTPs\n- API keys\n- OAuth\n- Password reset\n- Account deletion\n\nThe gateway quietly routes the request to the correct region. OAuth URLs, cookies and issuer URLs remain canonical instead of changing by region.\n\nAccount deletion also became safer:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[Account active] --> B[Mark account as deleting]\n    B --> C[Perform deletion]\n    C -->|Success| D[Mark permanently deleted]\n    C -->|Failure| A\n```\n\nThis prevents two servers from deleting or restoring the same account concurrently.\n\nThe shortest summary is:\n\n- **Before:** the client helped choose the region.\n- **Now:** the client always uses one URL; the gateway chooses the region.\n- **DATA-1:** routes tenant data safely.\n- **AUTH-1:** routes identity and authentication safely.\n- **During a move:** traffic stops briefly instead of allowing two regions to become owners.\n- **Old behavior still exists:** DataFn’s placement mode is optional, and AuthFn’s direct regional mode remains available.","started_at":1788005941,"completed_at":1788005987,"duration_ms":45979,"time_to_first_token_ms":6718}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:21:06.540Z","ordinal":12172,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"thread_settings_applied","thread_settings":{"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","model_provider_id":"openai","service_tier":"priority","approval_policy":"never","approvals_reviewer":"user","permission_profile":{"type":"disabled"},"active_permission_profile":{"id":":danger-full-access"},"cwd":"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev","reasoning_effort":"xhigh","reasoning_summary":"detailed","personality":"friendly","collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","settings":{"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","reasoning_effort":"xhigh","developer_instructions":"# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nUse the `request_user_input` tool only when it is listed in the available tools for this turn.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n"}}}}}
