e signals:\n    47\t- The user asked “Is it possible to make filefn client also accept the same http transport…” -> future answers should distinguish current capability from proposed API shape.\n    48\t- The user asked for a straight answer, not implementation details -> future answers should say “yes/no, with caveat” up front.\n    49\t\n    50\tKey steps:\n    51\t- Checked FileFn client source plus shared HTTP auth types and DataFn’s transport implementation.\n    52\t- Verified that the shared transport auth abstraction already exists in `packages/http` (`HttpTransportAuthProvider`, `getRequestHeaders`, `getCredentials`, `onUnauthorized`).\n    53\t- Verified that DataFn already uses this abstraction directly, so FileFn can align to the same shape without inventing a new auth model.\n    54\t\n    55\tFailures and how to do differently:\n    56\t- Avoid implying all FileFn network calls can use the same auth object; signed storage uploads are a separate fetch path and should not automatically inherit app auth headers.\n    57\t\n    58\tReusable knowledge:\n    59\t- The shared `@superfunctions/http` transport auth shape is already the common contract across the repo, so FileFn can accept it by adding an `auth`/transport option and plumbing it through the centralized request helper.\n    60\t- FileFn should keep the existing `getAuthHeaders` path as a compatibility alias if the API is expanded.\n    61\t- AuthFn’s `createTransportAuth()` is the exact factory shape that can feed that shared transport auth contract.\n    62\t- Presigned upload PUTs in FileFn are intentionally separate from API requests; any AuthFn transport integration should apply to FileFn API requests, not blindly to third-party signed URLs.\n    63\t\n    64\tReferences:\n    65\t- [1] `packages/http/src/types.ts:59-87` proves the shared transport auth provider API already exists (`getRequestHeaders`, `getCredentials`, `onUnauthorized`).\n    66\t- [2] `datafn/client/src/transport/http.ts:13-18,147-163` proves DataFn already accepts that shared `auth` provider and merges headers/credentials centrally.\n    67\t- [3] `authfn/client/src/types.ts:174-175` proves AuthFn clients already expose `createTransportAuth(...)`.\n    68\t- [4] `authfn/client/src/regional-client.ts:180-182` proves regional AuthFn clients forward `createTransportAuth(input)` from the underlying client.\n    69\t- [5] `filefn/client/src/client.ts:302-405` proves FileFn has two distinct fetch paths: authenticated API requests through `request()` and presigned URL uploads via raw `fetch()`.\n    70\t- [6] `filefn/client/src/client.ts:390-405` proves the signed-URL PUT path bypasses the main request/auth helper and therefore needs special handling if auth plumbing is added.\n"},"started_at_ms":1788006173972,"completed_at_ms":1788006173972}}
{"timestamp":"2026-08-29T12:22:53.976Z","ordinal":12214,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a04d79-0d18-70c3-8963-c3e596705fb4","call_id":"call_Af2u2dQRS7m0a5uplmOt19tS","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"     1\tthread_id: 019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56\n     2\tupdated_at: 2026-06-24T06:37:57+00:00\n     3\trollout_path: /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/06/24/rollout-2026-06-24T12-02-03-019ef854-1ea0-7d31-90cb-fed7430b6a56.jsonl\n     4\tcwd: /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev\n     5\tgit_branch: dev\n     6\t\n     7\t# Read-only source comparison of auth handling in Nucleus DataFn vs FileFn\n     8\t\n     9\tRollout context: The user asked how auth is handled for `datafn` in `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus/client/stores/datafn.store.ts` versus the FileFn demo page in `filefn/examples/production/client/src/routes/+page.svelte`, then followed up asking whether FileFn could accept the same HTTP transport/auth shape used by AuthFn/DataFn. The work stayed read-only and was grounded in current source.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Task 1: Compare current DataFn auth vs FileFn demo auth\n    12\t\n    13\tOutcome: success\n    14\t\n    15\tPreference signals:\n    16\t- The user asked a direct source-grounded comparison and then said “Just answer” / “Is it possible… Just answer” -> future responses in this area should stay concise and answer from current code instead of broad design speculation.\n    17\t- The user pointed to specific files, including `datafn.store.ts` and `+page.svelte` -> future comparisons should inspect exact call sites rather than infer from package names.\n    18\t\n    19\tKey steps:\n    20\t- Inspected the response-formatting skill and then read `client/stores/datafn.store.ts`, `client/components/account/auth.ts`, and the FileFn demo page.\n    21\t- Verified that Nucleus DataFn builds HTTP transport auth through `createNucleumDatafnHttpOptions()` and `createNucleumAuthFnTransportAuth()`, while the demo page hardcodes `Authorization: Bearer demo-token` via `getAuthHeaders`.\n    22\t- Traced the DataFn auth gating: remote HTTP auth is only created for sync-eligible, online initialization; local-only mode uses no remote auth.\n    23\t\n    24\tFailures and how to do differently:\n    25\t- A broad `rg` across the workspace produced many irrelevant auth hits; narrowing to the exact store/helper files was the useful pivot.\n    26\t- Do not collapse FileFn’s upload client auth and its presigned URL fetches into one path; the code has separate request flows.\n    27\t\n    28\tReusable knowledge:\n    29\t- `client/stores/datafn.store.ts` is the current Nucleus DataFn runtime entrypoint, and `client/components/account/auth.ts` is the session-backed transport auth helper.\n    30\t- Nucleus DataFn transport auth is AuthFn-backed and conditional: `shouldUseAuthFnBearerSession()` returns true only in native embed / extension browser contexts, so web defaults to cookie-session auth and hybrid bearer/cookie is reserved for those contexts.\n    31\t- FileFn’s example client currently uses a much simpler auth surface: `FileFnClientConfig.getAuthHeaders?: () => Promise<Record<string, string>> | Record<string, string>` and `apiFetch()` manually merges those headers.\n    32\t- FileFn client has many request methods that all go through a central `request()` helper, but presigned upload URL PUTs bypass that helper and call `fetch()` directly.\n    33\t\n    34\tReferences:\n    35\t- [1] `client/stores/datafn.store.ts:522-637` proves Nucleus DataFn only creates remote HTTP auth during sync-eligible initialization and wires `sync` / `sync-direct` with `http` options.\n    36\t- [2] `client/stores/datafn.store.ts:847-864` proves DataFn HTTP transport auth is created through `createNucleumAuthFnTransportAuth(...)` and supports a public-link plugin.\n    37\t- [3] `client/components/account/auth.ts:37-83` proves AuthFn transport auth is regional, cookie-based by default, and bearer-token hybrid only in native embed / extension contexts.\n    38\t- [4] `filefn/examples/production/client/src/routes/+page.svelte:8-46` proves the demo page hardcodes `Bearer demo-token` and reuses it manually in `apiFetch()`.\n    39\t- [5] `filefn/client/src/types.ts:13-16` proves FileFn client config currently exposes only `getAuthHeaders` (not a shared transport auth provider).\n    40\t- [6] `filefn/client/src/client.ts:183-215` proves FileFn’s central request path only merges `getAuthHeaders` before `fetch()`.\n    41\t\n    42\t## Task 2: Judge whether FileFn can accept the same HTTP transport/auth shape\n    43\t\n    44\tOutcome: success\n    45\t\n    46\tPreference signals:\n    47\t- The user asked “Is it possible to make filefn client also accept the same http transport…” -> future answers should distinguish current capability from proposed API shape.\n    48\t- The user asked for a straight answer, not implementation details -> future answers should say “yes/no, with caveat” up front.\n    49\t\n    50\tKey steps:\n    51\t- Checked FileFn client source plus shared HTTP auth types and DataFn’s transport implementation.\n    52\t- Verified that the shared transport auth abstraction already exists in `packages/http` (`HttpTransportAuthProvider`, `getRequestHeaders`, `getCredentials`, `onUnauthorized`).\n    53\t- Verified that DataFn already uses this abstraction directly, so FileFn can align to the same shape without inventing a new auth model.\n    54\t\n    55\tFailures and how to do differently:\n    56\t- Avoid implying all FileFn network calls can use the same auth object; signed storage uploads are a separate fetch path and should not automatically inherit app auth headers.\n    57\t\n    58\tReusable knowledge:\n    59\t- The shared `@superfunctions/http` transport auth shape is already the common contract across the repo, so FileFn can accept it by adding an `auth`/transport option and plumbing it through the centralized request helper.\n    60\t- FileFn should keep the existing `getAuthHeaders` path as a compatibility alias if the API is expanded.\n    61\t- AuthFn’s `createTransportAuth()` is the exact factory shape that can feed that shared transport auth contract.\n    62\t- Presigned upload PUTs in FileFn are intentionally separate from API requests; any AuthFn transport integration should apply to FileFn API requests, not blindly to third-party signed URLs.\n    63\t\n    64\tReferences:\n    65\t- [1] `packages/http/src/types.ts:59-87` proves the shared transport auth provider API already exists (`getRequestHeaders`, `getCredentials`, `onUnauthorized`).\n    66\t- [2] `datafn/client/src/transport/http.ts:13-18,147-163` proves DataFn already accepts that shared `auth` provider and merges headers/credentials centrally.\n    67\t- [3] `authfn/client/src/types.ts:174-175` proves AuthFn clients already expose `createTransportAuth(...)`.\n    68\t- [4] `authfn/client/src/regional-client.ts:180-182` proves regional AuthFn clients forward `createTransportAuth(input)` from the underlying client.\n    69\t- [5] `filefn/client/src/client.ts:302-405` proves FileFn has two distinct fetch paths: authenticated API requests through `request()` and presigned URL uploads via raw `fetch()`.\n    70\t- [6] `filefn/client/src/client.ts:390-405` proves the signed-URL PUT path bypasses the main request/auth helper and therefore needs special handling if auth plumbing is added.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a04d78-7145-7893-8452-63cdfa1be403","create_time":1788006173.976683}}}
